  - Description: Genetic testing may be done through a genetic counselor, who helps patients understand the results
  - Source: GENETIC COUNSELOR
  - Target: GENETIC TESTING
  - Description: A genetic counselor helps interpret the results of genetic tests</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GERMLINE MUTATION">
      <data key="d0">GERMLINE MUTATION</data>
      <data key="d1">GERMLINE MUTATION</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A germline mutation is a gene mutation inherited from a birth parent. It is an inherited mutation present in every cell of the body and is passed down in families, which is why it is also called a hereditary mutation. Germline mutations can increase an individual's cancer risk, can be passed on to children, and may also be present in blood relatives.
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC CANCER RISK TESTING
  - Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Description: Genetic cancer risk testing looks for germline mutations inherited from birth parents.
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Target: HEREDITARY GENE TESTING
  - Description: Hereditary gene testing looks for inherited germline mutations in every cell</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOMATIC MUTATION">
      <data key="d0">SOMATIC MUTATION</data>
      <data key="d1">SOMATIC MUTATION</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Somatic mutation refers to a genetic change that arises from an error in cell division during tissue growth, occurring spontaneously at some point later in life rather than being inherited from birth parents. Also known as an acquired mutation, it develops in only some cells of the body, not all, and can result from random chance or exposure to environmental factors. These mutations are not passed on to offspring, as they occur in non-germline cells.  
Relationships:  
  - Source: MUTATION  
  - Target: SOMATIC MUTATION  
  - Description: A somatic mutation is an acquired type of mutation.  
  - Source: SOMATIC MUTATION  
  - Target: MUTATION  
  - Description: Somatic mutation is a type of mutation acquired later in life in some cells  
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION  
  - Target: SOMATIC MUTATION  
  - Description: Germline and somatic mutations are the two described categories of mutations; one is inherited, the other acquired  
  - Source: GENETIC TEST  
  - Target: SOMATIC MUTATION  
  - Description: Genetic testing can detect somatic mutations that arise later in life.  
  - Source: SOMATIC MUTATION  
  - Target: TUMOR TESTING  
  - Description: Tumor testing detects somatic mutations or abnormalities in tumor DNA and DNA repair genes  
  - Source: SOMATIC MUTATION  
  - Target: EWING SARCOMA  
  - Description: The gene fusions in Ewing sarcoma result from somatic mutation or errors of cell division, not from inherited mutations.  

---</data>
    </node>
