Description: DIARRHEA is a common side effect associated with several cancer treatment modalities. Specifically, it frequently occurs as a result of external beam radiation therapy, particularly when radiation is directed at the abdomen or pelvis. Additionally, diarrhea is a well-documented adverse effect of targeted therapy, a class of drugs designed to interfere with specific molecules involved in tumor growth. Furthermore, the chemotherapy agent irinotecan is known to commonly cause diarrhea as a side effect. In summary, diarrhea is a shared and clinically significant side effect across external beam radiation therapy, targeted therapy, and treatment with irinotecan.
Relationships: None listed.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIDE EFFECTS">
      <data key="d0">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d1">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">SIDE EFFECTS are unintended effects that can arise from medical treatment, most notably external beam radiation therapy (EBRT). Common adverse effects of EBRT include fatigue, skin redness and irritation, diarrhea, nausea, and frequent or painful urination. These effects may be monitored during treatment—for example, heart tests can be used to track treatment-related side effects—and supportive care is often provided to address and manage them. Together, these descriptions highlight that side effects are both a known set of physical reactions to radiation therapy and a broader concept encompassing any unintended consequences of treatment that require monitoring and supportive intervention.
Relationships: None listed.</data>
