    <node id="PERFORMANCE STATUS">
      <data key="d0">PERFORMANCE STATUS</data>
      <data key="d1">PERFORMANCE STATUS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Performance status (PS) is a clinical rating of a person’s general level of fitness and overall functional ability, specifically reflecting how well they can perform ordinary day-to-day activities and self-care tasks. It is commonly measured using the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) scale, which provides a standardized scoring system for a patient’s current physical capabilities. Performance status is used by the care team as part of the patient workup to help guide treatment decisions, including determining the appropriate treatment level and eligibility for clinical trials. Thus, it serves as a key indicator of a patient’s ability to carry out daily activities and informs the selection of a personalized treatment plan.
Relationships:
  - Source: PERFORMANCE STATUS
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team uses performance status to gauge how much treatment the patient can handle.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: PERFORMANCE STATUS
  - Description: Performance status is considered when choosing a treatment plan for cancer patients
  - Source: PERFORMANCE STATUS
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: Performance status helps determine whether the patient may be eligible for a clinical trial.
  - Source: DIAGNOSING ESOPHAGEAL CANCER
  - Target: PERFORMANCE STATUS
  - Description: Performance status is assessed during the workup for esophageal cancer
  - Source: TESTING FOR BREAST CANCER
  - Target: PERFORMANCE STATUS
  - Description: Performance status is used as part of testing and treatment planning for breast cancer.
  - Source: EASTERN COOPERATIVE ONCOLOGY GROUP
  - Target: PERFORMANCE STATUS
  - Description: Performance status is rated using the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) scale</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
