    <node id="PROGRESSION">
      <data key="d0">PROGRESSION</data>
      <data key="d1">PROGRESSION</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">PROGRESSION refers to the advancement or worsening of cancer during or after treatment. Specifically, it is the growth of cancer that occurs either while a patient is undergoing chemotherapy or after chemotherapy has been completed. When this type of progression happens in the context of chemotherapy, immunotherapy may be recommended as a subsequent treatment option. In essence, progression indicates that the cancer has not been adequately controlled by the current therapy, prompting a shift in treatment strategy, often toward immunotherapy.
Relationships:
  - Source: PROGRESSION
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Cancer progression can occur during or after chemotherapy treatment.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: PROGRESSION
  - Description: Progression refers to cancer growing during or after chemotherapy treatment
  - Source: METASTASIS
  - Target: PROGRESSION
  - Description: Metastatic cancer may progress during or after chemotherapy
  - Source: PROGRESSION
  - Target: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Description: For patients whose cancer shows progression either during or after chemotherapy, immunotherapy is recommended as the next line of treatment for those who are eligible. This approach applies when the disease advances despite or following completion of chemotherapy, establishing immunotherapy as the standard subsequent therapeutic option.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
