    <node id="HEAD AND NECK CANCER">
      <data key="d0">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">HEAD AND NECK CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Head and neck cancer is a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck region, encompassing malignancies of the oral cavity, oropharynx, nasopharynx, larynx, hypopharynx, and nasal cavity/paranasal sinuses. This group includes mouth cancer, and its development is strongly associated with tobacco and alcohol use as key risk factors. The diagnosis and treatment of head and neck cancer can have wide-ranging effects on a patient's health and quality of life. Treatment may impact the jaw, teeth, and gums, as well as overall oral health. Beyond the oral cavity, it can also affect endocrine glands, hearing, nutrition, speech, swallowing, fertility, and overall performance status. Thus, head and neck cancer represents a complex set of diseases requiring comprehensive care that addresses both the tumor and the multiple functional systems that may be compromised by the disease and its management.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Description: Head and neck cancer is a broad category of cancers that affect regions including the oral cavity, throat, larynx, sinuses, and salivary glands. Nasopharyngeal cancer is a specific type of head and neck cancer that arises in the nasopharynx, which is the upper part of the throat located behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. As a subtype of head and neck cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer shares the general characteristics of this disease group but is distinguished by its anatomical origin. Both descriptions confirm that nasopharyngeal cancer originates specifically in the nasopharynx and falls under the classification of head and neck cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CARCINOMA
  - Target: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a type of head and neck cancer.

---</data>
    </node>
