    <node id="NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER">
      <data key="d0">NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Nasopharyngeal cancer (NPC), also known as nasopharyngeal carcinoma, is a type of head and neck cancer that arises in the nasopharynx—the upper part of the throat behind the nose, near the nasal cavity. It is considered a rare cancer overall, but it occurs most frequently in people from parts of Asia, Native America, and North Africa. The most common form of NPC is squamous cell carcinoma. Patient information for nasopharyngeal cancer is available through the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN).
Relationships:
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Description: Head and neck cancer is a broad category of cancers that affect regions including the oral cavity, throat, larynx, sinuses, and salivary glands. Nasopharyngeal cancer is a specific type of head and neck cancer that arises in the nasopharynx, which is the upper part of the throat located behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. As a subtype of head and neck cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer shares the general characteristics of this disease group but is distinguished by its anatomical origin. Both descriptions confirm that nasopharyngeal cancer originates specifically in the nasopharynx and falls under the classification of head and neck cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of cancer in the nasopharynx.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA
  - Description: Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: THROAT CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer affects the nasopharynx, an upper part of the throat, so it is a type of throat cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: ASIA
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer most often occurs in people from parts of Asia.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: NATIVE AMERICA
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer most often occurs in people from Native America.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: NORTH AFRICA
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer most often occurs in people from North Africa.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: ESOPHAGEAL CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer should not be confused with esophageal cancer, another type of cancer affecting the throat.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: LARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer should not be confused with laryngeal cancer, another type of cancer affecting the throat.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: OROPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer and oropharyngeal cancer are cancers of adjacent parts of the pharynx.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: HYPOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer and hypopharyngeal cancer are cancers of different parts of the pharynx.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: TESTING FOR NPC
  - Description: Testing for NPC is the diagnostic workup for nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: TEST RESULTS
  - Description: Test results are the findings used to determine treatment for nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: TREATMENT PLANNING
  - Description: Treatment planning is for nasopharyngeal cancer and uses testing results.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Biopsy is used to evaluate nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: IMAGING TESTS
  - Description: Imaging tests are used in the workup for nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Description: Biomarker testing is used in the workup for nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: GENERAL HEALTH TESTS
  - Description: General health tests are performed as part of the workup for nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history is collected when evaluating nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAMINATION
  - Description: Physical examination is performed when evaluating nasopharyngeal cancer.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Description: NCCN provides more information on nasopharyngeal cancer at NCCN.org/patientguidelines and in the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app. This nasopharyngeal cancer patient guide is provided through NCCN patient education resources.

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEST RESULTS">
      <data key="d0">TEST RESULTS</data>
      <data key="d1">TEST RESULTS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">TEST RESULTS are the consolidated findings obtained through a comprehensive diagnostic workup for nasopharyngeal cancer. These results integrate information gathered from multiple sources, including the patient's medical history, a physical examination, blood tests, imaging studies, and a biopsy. Medical history and physical examination provide initial clinical context and identify suspicious signs or symptoms, while blood tests offer additional laboratory data to support the diagnosis. Imaging studies help visualize the extent and location of the tumor, and a biopsy provides definitive tissue confirmation of malignancy. Altogether, these test results are the critical outcomes that determine the treatment plan for nasopharyngeal cancer, guiding clinical decisions regarding therapy. The information from all these diagnostic components is synthesized to establish the stage of the disease and tailor the most appropriate management strategy for the patient.
Relationships:
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: TEST RESULTS
  - Description: Test results are the findings used to determine treatment for nasopharyngeal cancer.

---</data>
    </node>
