    <node id="NEUROSURGEON">
      <data key="d0">NEUROSURGEON</data>
      <data key="d1">NEUROSURGEON</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A neurosurgeon is a physician and surgical specialist who performs surgical procedures for brain tumors, including both resections and stereotactic biopsies. They perform brain biopsy procedures—encompassing both stereotactic and open biopsies—in an operating room. During these procedures, the neurosurgeon makes openings in the skull, uses surgical instruments and navigation systems to accurately target the tumor, and receives lab results during the operation to determine how much tumor to remove. This role combines expertise in open surgical techniques with minimally invasive stereotactic approaches, allowing for precise diagnosis and treatment of brain lesions.
Relationships: (No direct relationships listed in the provided data)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROPATHOLOGIST">
      <data key="d0">NEUROPATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d1">NEUROPATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A specialist who examines biopsy tissue under a microscope, performs histologic and molecular testing, sends results back to the neurosurgeon, and provides information used by the treatment team.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: NEUROPATHOLOGIST
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: The neuropathologist examines the tissue sample obtained from a biopsy.
  - Source: NEUROPATHOLOGIST
