    <node id="RISK ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d0">RISK ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">RISK ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Risk assessment is a systematic process used to identify potential problems, evaluate contributing factors, and consider possible outcomes in order to guide clinical decision-making. Across different medical contexts, it serves as a foundational tool for determining disease risk, classifying severity, and selecting appropriate screening or treatment strategies.

In the context of prostate cancer, risk assessment is employed to determine an individual’s likelihood of having or developing the disease. It involves analyzing clinical information—such as PSA levels, biopsy results, and other patient factors—to gauge the aggressiveness of the cancer and guide treatment decisions. This allows clinicians to tailor management plans based on the predicted course of the disease.

For cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), risk assessment classifies the disease into categories of low-risk, high-risk, or very high-risk. This classification is based on tumor features, including size, depth, location, histologic characteristics, and other high-risk factors such as immunosuppression or perineural invasion. The resulting risk categorization directly informs treatment choices, helping to determine the need for wider surgical excision, adjuvant radiation, or systemic therapy.

In lung cancer, risk assessment focuses on evaluating a person’s risk factors—such as age, smoking history, and exposure to carcinogens—and calculating the chance of developing the disease. This assessment is used to decide whether an individual is appropriate for lung cancer screening, typically with low-dose CT, ensuring that screening is offered to those who are most likely to benefit while avoiding unnecessary procedures in low-risk individuals.

Overall, risk assessment is a versatile and essential component of modern oncology. It integrates patient-specific and tumor-specific data to stratify risk, personalize care, and optimize outcomes across prostate cancer, cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, and lung cancer. While the specific parameters and decision thresholds vary by disease, the underlying principle remains consistent: to identify potential health risks early and match the intensity of intervention to the level of threat posed by the condition.
Relationships:
  - Source: RISK ASSESSMENT
  - Target: RISK GROUP CLASSIFICATION
  - Description: Risk groups are used by care providers to assess risk
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: RISK ASSESSMENT
  - Description: Risk assessment helps decide whether surgery is recommended based on the risk level of CSCC.
  - Source: RISK ASSESSMENT
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Risk assessment determines whether treatment is needed and what the treatment plan will be
  - Source: RISK ASSESSMENT
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: A risk assessment considers how likely prostate cancer is to remain within or spread outside the prostate and whether it may recur
  - Source: LUNG CANCER SCREENING
  - Target: RISK ASSESSMENT
  - Description: Risk assessment determines whether a patient qualifies for lung cancer screening
  - Source: MOLECULAR BIOMARKER TEST
  - Target: RISK ASSESSMENT
  - Description: Molecular biomarker tests are one of the tools used in risk assessment</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI">
      <data key="d0">MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">MRI</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a noninvasive imaging test that uses a powerful magnetic field, radio waves, and a computer to create detailed pictures of areas inside the body. Unlike CT scans, it does not use x-rays or ionizing radiation. The MRI machine is typically a large, enclosed tunnel, though open MRI systems are also available. A contrast agent (dye) may be injected to make tissues, organs, and blood vessels show up more clearly. MRI can be combined with PET imaging for additional diagnostic information.

MRI is widely used to evaluate soft tissues throughout the body. It is the most useful test for identifying a glioma and is used to visualize the brain and central nervous system, including for diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma. It is also used for breast imaging, where images are interpreted by a breast diagnostic radiologist, and for spine imaging. MRI can evaluate the bladder and urinary tract to check for cancer spread, and it may be used when ultrasound images are unclear. In rectal cancer, pelvic MRI shows how deeply the tumor has grown into the rectal wall and whether it has spread to nearby lymph nodes.

In oncology, MRI plays a key role in cervical cancer management. It is used as an initial imaging test to determine the extent of disease before treatment, with and without contrast to examine the primary tumor and neck. It is also used for pelvic scanning after fertility-sparing treatment or as additional follow-up for stages 2–4A cervical cancer. MRI of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast can serve as an alternative to CT in surveillance. Additionally, MRI can be used to guide a biopsy.

While MRI is highly detailed and avoids radiation, it may be unsuitable for people with certain pacemakers, heart monitors, or surgical clips due to the strong magnetic field. Scan times are generally longer than CT. Overall, MRI is a versatile and powerful diagnostic tool for imaging the brain, spine, breast, pelvis, abdomen, and other soft-tissue structures across many cancer types and neurological conditions.
Relationships:
  - Source: MRI
  - Target: BRAIN MRI
  - Description: Brain MRI is an MRI application for the brain
  - Source: MRI
  - Target: SPINE MRI
  - Description: Spine MRI is an MRI application for the spine

---</data>
    </node>
