    <node id="PATHOLOGIC STAGING">
      <data key="d0">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Pathologic staging, also referred to as surgical staging, is the post-surgical process of determining the extent to which cancer has spread. It is based on laboratory findings from tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery. The stage is assigned by a pathologist who examines the surgically removed specimens, such as a lymph node, to rate the cancer. This staging method is also known as the surgical stage and is designated with categories such as pN (pathologic node stage). Pathologic staging provides more precise information about the cancer than clinical staging because it is derived from direct examination of actual tissue. It is commonly used for various cancers, including ovarian cancer, where the post-surgery determination of how far the disease has spread relies on information gained during surgery and surgical staging.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
