**ENTITY: IMAGING TESTS**
Description: Imaging tests are diagnostic procedures that create pictures of the inside of the body to evaluate cancer. They are used across many cancer types to diagnose, stage, and guide treatment. The tests can reveal the presence, size, location, and shape of a primary tumor, and determine whether cancer has spread to nearby tissues, organs, lymph nodes, or distant parts of the body. This information is essential for clinical staging, assessing tumor spread, and planning treatment.

Common imaging modalities include X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), FDG-PET/CT, ultrasound, mammograms, and bone scans. Some tests, such as transrectal ultrasound or MRI, are used specifically to see if cancer has spread beyond the prostate, and may not be needed for early-stage prostate cancer. Mammograms create pictures of the breast and are used in evaluating breast cancer and determining treatment planning. Ultrasound can be used to diagnose and confirm liver cancer. CT, MRI, and FDG-PET/CT are used to image tumors of the head and neck, such as nasopharyngeal carcinoma, helping determine stage and treatment plan. Imaging tests also play a role in ovarian cancer by showing tumor location, size, and shape, and whether it has spread beyond the ovaries before surgery. For bladder cancer, scans show whether the cancer has spread to nearby tissues, organs, lymph nodes, or distant parts of the body. For esophageal cancer, imaging tests are used to diagnose and determine the extent of the disease. In leukemia, imaging tests such as CT, MRI, and PET scans are used to look for leukemia outside the bone marrow, as well as areas of infection or bleeding.

Imaging tests are used during the initial workup and also during follow-up and surveillance to evaluate whether cancer has returned. A radiologist typically interprets the images and sends a report to the patient's doctor. Some imaging tests require contrast material and specific preparation. Overall, these tests are indispensable in cancer care for detecting cancer deposits, determining the extent of disease, and informing treatment decisions. By taking pictures of the inside of the body, imaging tests help the care team stage cancer, plan treatment, and monitor patients over time. They are performed in a variety of clinical settings, from initial diagnosis through long-term follow-up, and are adapted to the specific cancer type and clinical question. Whether assessing a primary tumor, checking for metastasis, or confirming a diagnosis, imaging tests provide critical visual information that guides medical decision-making. Their results are used in treatment planning for many cancers, including breast, nasopharyngeal, ovarian, bladder, esophageal, liver, and prostate cancers. In summary, imaging tests are a cornerstone of modern oncology, enabling precise evaluation of cancer and its spread.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: TNM SCORES**
Description: Scores for Tumor, Node, and Metastasis that are part of cancer staging.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: IMAGING**
Description: IMAGING refers to the use of diagnostic tests and scans to produce detailed pictures of the inside of the body for cancer evaluation. It includes a wide range of modalities such as X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), PET-CT, ultrasound (including transvaginal and endorectal), mammography, and bone scans. Imaging is interpreted by a radiologist and helps reveal a tumor’s location, size, and characteristics, determine whether cancer has spread beyond the primary site, guide treatment decisions, and monitor for recurrence or response to therapy.

In initial cancer staging, imaging is used to determine the extent of disease before treatment. For cervical cancer, initial imaging may include CT, MRI, PET, and/or transvaginal ultrasound. For prostate cancer, imaging tests are used alongside PSA tests and digital rectal exams to provide a more complete picture of the prostate and help decide whether a biopsy is needed. Imaging also helps determine whether prostate cancer has spread. For colon cancer, CT with contrast is the main test used to assess the extent of disease in the body. In primary CNS lymphoma, MRI or CT scans are used to detect tumors and aid diagnosis. In some cases, imaging is also used to evaluate basal cell skin cancer.

After treatment, imaging plays a key role in evaluating symptoms or suspected recurrence. For cervical cancer, post-treatment imaging may include CT, MRI, and/or PET/CT scans, typically from the neck to the midthigh, if recurrence or spread is suspected. However, routine radiologic imaging is generally not needed at regular intervals after cervical cancer treatment unless there is a clinical suspicion of recurrence. In contrast, for stage II and III anal cancers in complete remission, radiologic imaging such as CT or MRI may be recommended every year for three years to check for spread. For rectal cancer after endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) or transanal local excision, surveillance imaging with endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI with contrast is recommended to check for recurrence. After treatment for recurrence, yearly imaging using CT and/or MRI may be performed.

Imaging results are often kept in a medical binder, and patients are advised to obtain copies of their imaging results for their records. Although the specific imaging modality and schedule vary by cancer type and clinical context, the overall purpose of imaging is to provide essential anatomical and functional information that guides cancer diagnosis, staging, and management.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Imaging and scans provide information used in tumor staging for prostate cancer

**ENTITY: SURGICAL STAGING**
Description: SURGICAL STAGING, also known as pathologic staging, is a procedure performed during surgery to determine the extent of cancer, most notably ovarian cancer. It is considered the most accurate method for staging ovarian cancer. During the procedure, a surgeon removes and tests lymph nodes and takes tissue samples from organs and areas where the cancer may have spread. Specifically, para-aortic lymph nodes are typically removed, while pelvic lymph nodes may or may not be removed depending on the case. This process allows a pathologist to assess tumor growth and lymph node involvement, ultimately assigning a cancer stage of I, II, or III. While the procedure is often minimally invasive, it is fundamentally a surgical intervention that provides critical information for treatment planning and prognosis.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: NPC STAGING**
Description: Another name for nasopharyngeal carcinoma staging; uses TNM scores and biomarker status.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER STAGING**
Description: Staging specifically for nasopharyngeal carcinoma, including tumor, node, metastasis, and biomarker testing.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: PATHOLOGIC STAGING**
Description: Pathologic staging, also referred to as surgical staging, is the post-surgical process of determining the extent to which cancer has spread. It is based on laboratory findings from tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery. The stage is assigned by a pathologist who examines the surgically removed specimens, such as a lymph node, to rate the cancer. This staging method is also known as the surgical stage and is designated with categories such as pN (pathologic node stage). Pathologic staging provides more precise information about the cancer than clinical staging because it is derived from direct examination of actual tissue. It is commonly used for various cancers, including ovarian cancer, where the post-surgery determination of how far the disease has spread relies on information gained during surgery and surgical staging.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: FIGO**
Description: FIGO is the organization whose staging system is used to stage cervical cancer
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: RECTAL CANCER**
Description: Rectal cancer is a malignant disease that forms in the rectum, which is the final section of the large intestine. As a cancer of the rectum, it is the focus of dedicated patient education materials: the NCCN Guidelines for Patients specifically address this disease and its treatment. These guidelines provide evidence-based information on managing rectal cancer, covering topics such as diagnosis, treatment options, and supportive care. The staging of rectal cancer ranges from stage I to stage III, and this classification is determined by two key factors: the extent of tumor growth into the rectal wall and the involvement of nearby lymph nodes. In summary, rectal cancer is a distinct cancer type with established clinical guidance for patients, and its staging system—based on local invasion and lymphatic spread—helps define the appropriate treatment approach.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS**
Description: The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) is a professional organization best known for developing the FIGO staging system, a standardized framework used to classify the extent of gynecological cancers. This system is widely adopted in clinical practice and is considered the most commonly used staging method for both ovarian cancer and cervical cancer. For ovarian cancer, the FIGO staging system is similar to the AJCC (American Joint Committee on Cancer) system, and it serves as the primary tool for determining disease progression and guiding treatment decisions. For cervical cancer, FIGO similarly provides the definitive staging criteria, ensuring consistent evaluation and communication among healthcare providers worldwide. By establishing these staging systems, FIGO has played a central role in advancing the diagnosis, treatment, and research of gynecologic malignancies. Its staging classifications are integral to oncology practice, enabling accurate prognosis and comparison of treatment outcomes across different clinical settings.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

**ENTITY: TESTING**
Description: TESTING is a comprehensive medical process used to diagnose cancer, determine its extent, plan treatment, and monitor patient health. It encompasses a wide range of procedures and assessments tailored to specific cancer types and clinical needs.

For determining the extent of cancer, TESTING includes imaging studies, cystoscopy, proctoscopy, and lymph node testing. These procedures help physicians evaluate whether and where cancer has spread, providing critical information for staging and treatment decisions.

In the context of basal cell skin cancer, TESTING is the process needed to diagnose and treat the disease, incorporating general health tests, biopsy, and imaging. Similarly, for regional cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), TESTING is used to determine whether and where cancer has spread, involving imaging, biopsy, and surgical evaluation. These approaches ensure accurate diagnosis and appropriate management of skin cancers.

TESTING also describes the overall timeline of the medical workup, which may take days or weeks for all test results to come in. This reflects the sequential nature of diagnostic procedures, from initial screening to confirmatory testing and staging.

For prostate cancer, TESTING represents the overall process of using prostate cancer screening tests, general health tests, and diagnostic tests. This multifaceted approach aims to detect cancer early, plan effective treatment, and monitor the patient’s health over time.

Across all contexts, TESTING serves as a foundational step in oncology, integrating various diagnostic tools such as imaging, biopsy, endoscopic examinations, and laboratory assessments. The specific combination of tests depends on the suspected or confirmed cancer type, the patient’s overall health, and the clinical questions being addressed. By gathering comprehensive data, TESTING enables healthcare providers to make informed decisions about treatment and follow-up care.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
