  - Description: Information gained during surgery contributes to determining the pathologic stage of ovarian cancer

ENTITY: SURGEON
Description: SURGEON is a medical professional who specializes in performing surgical procedures. As a physician, the surgeon may also provide cancer genetics counseling and perform diagnostic procedures such as skin biopsies. In the context of cancer care, the surgeon is skilled in identifying and locating sentinel lymph nodes, which are key to determining whether cancer has spread. Thus, the surgeon’s role combines operative expertise with diagnostic and counseling responsibilities in patient care.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: ROUTINE EYE SURGERY
Description: Routine eye surgery is an operation performed by an eye surgeon that can treat cataracts caused by radiation therapy.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: LAPAROTOMY
Description: A traditional surgery performed through a large abdominal incision
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: SURGICAL EVALUATION
Description: Surgical evaluation uses the results of testing to decide whether a patient is a candidate for surgery.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: MINIMALLY INVASIVE SURGERY
Description: Laparoscopic surgery involving a few small cuts, with less pain, scarring, and recovery time than laparotomy
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: ANOTHER SURGERY
Description: A second operation preferred if a concerning amount of cancer remains after initial surgery.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: CLINICAL PRE-SURGERY STAGE
Description: Clinical pre-surgery stage is the best estimate of the extent of rectal cancer based on test results before surgery; it is used to determine treatment options.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: COLECTOMY
Description: COLECTOMY is a surgical procedure involving the removal of part or all of the colon. It is the primary surgical treatment for non-metastatic colon cancer, performed to excise the cancerous portion of the colon along with nearby lymph nodes, which are then tested to determine the extent of disease. This surgery is typically done before decisions about adjuvant chemotherapy are made. The procedure can be performed in several ways: as a one-stage operation where healthy colon ends are reconnected; with a colostomy, where the bowel is diverted to an opening in the abdominal wall; or after stenting to unblock the bowel. Potential side effects and complications include anastomotic leaks, changes in bowel habits, bowel obstruction due to scar tissue, and hernia formation.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: ABDOMINAL SURGERY
Description: Surgery performed through an abdominal incision, generally preferred for larger stage 1B2 tumors
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: EXPLORATORY SURGERY
Description: Exploratory surgery is a procedure to look inside the body and determine the extent of recurrent cervical cancer, helping guide treatment.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: COMPLICATION
Description: An unwanted and unplanned result from a procedure; biopsy complications may include infection, rectal bleeding, or blood in urine, stool, or semen.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: APR
Description: Abdominoperineal resection; a surgery during which cancerous lymph nodes may be removed
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: NEOADJUVANT THERAPY
Description: Systemic drug treatment given before surgery; also called preoperative therapy
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: FERTILITY-SPARING SURGERY
Description: Surgical procedure that preserves fertility by not removing the uterus or ovaries
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: PDEMA/MOHS SURGERY
Description: A type of surgery for squamous cell skin cancer listed in the text among surgical treatment options.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: SURGICAL MENOPAUSE
Description: SURGICAL MENOPAUSE is menopause caused by surgery, specifically the sudden drop in estrogen that follows the removal of both ovaries. Unlike natural menopause, which occurs gradually, surgical menopause triggers abrupt and often severe symptoms. It is frequently referenced as a comparison point when describing the symptoms of radiation-induced menopause, as both involve an induced cessation of ovarian function.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: TRANSABDOMINAL SURGERY
Description: Transabdominal surgery, also called abdominal surgery or transabdominal resection, is a surgical approach that involves cutting into the abdomen to remove a rectal tumor, surrounding healthy tissue, and nearby lymph nodes. After the procedure, the removed tissue and lymph nodes are sent to a laboratory for testing. This surgery is recommended for certain rectal cancer cases, including T2 tumors, some T1 tumors, and some larger T3 cancers that have not spread to lymph nodes. It is also performed after local excision when high-risk features or muscle invasion are identified. During transabdominal surgery, at least 12 lymph nodes should be removed for proper staging and evaluation.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)

ENTITY: COLD KNIFE CONIZATION
Description: (No description provided in the data)
