    <node id="THROAT CANCER">
      <data key="d0">THROAT CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">THROAT CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Throat cancer is a general term used to describe a group of cancers that affect the throat and nearby structures. It encompasses several distinct types, including oropharyngeal cancer, laryngeal cancer, hypopharyngeal cancer, esophageal cancer, and nasopharyngeal cancer. As a category, throat cancer is classified as a head and neck cancer. One notable subtype, oropharyngeal cancer, is most often related to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection. Throat cancer is also the subject of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Throat Cancer, which provide patient-focused information on diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care. Overall, throat cancer represents a diverse set of malignancies with varying risk factors, presentations, and treatment approaches, unified by their location in the throat region.
Relationships:
  - Source: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER
  - Target: THROAT CANCER
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer affects the nasopharynx, an upper part of the throat, so it is a type of throat cancer.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
