    <node id="TREATMENT PLANNING">
      <data key="d0">TREATMENT PLANNING</data>
      <data key="d1">TREATMENT PLANNING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">TREATMENT PLANNING is the process of developing and deciding the course of cancer treatment. It begins with testing and evaluation, using results from imaging, biopsy, and other diagnostic assessments to inform the plan. These test results, along with the established diagnosis, are used to determine the appropriate treatment approach. In essence, treatment planning starts with testing and relies on the findings to shape the overall strategy for cancer care.
Relationships:
  - Source: TEST RESULTS
  - Target: TREATMENT PLANNING
  - Description: TEST RESULTS play a critical role in TREATMENT PLANNING, as they are used to determine the appropriate course of care for a patient. Specifically, test results derived from a patient's medical history, physical examination, imaging studies, and biopsy collectively inform and guide the treatment plan. In this way, comprehensive diagnostic information is synthesized to ensure that treatment decisions are based on a thorough evaluation of the patient's condition.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFECTIOUS MONONUCLEOSIS">
