    <node id="BLADDER CANCER">
      <data key="d0">BLADDER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">BLADDER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Bladder cancer is a malignancy that originates in the tissues of the bladder. It is the main topic of the provided text and is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the bladder lining. Urothelial carcinoma, the most common type of bladder cancer, shares many clinical and pathological features with bladder cancer more broadly, and the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) publishes patient guidelines specifically for bladder cancer to support diagnosis, treatment, and management decisions. Several risk factors and biological features are associated with the disease: smoking is known to increase the risk of developing bladder cancer, and germline mutations—inherited genetic changes—are found in some cases of the disease. Overall, bladder cancer encompasses a range of presentations, with shared features with urothelial carcinoma, recognized genetic and environmental contributors, and dedicated clinical guidance from major oncology organizations.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

---</data>
