    <node id="HYSTERECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d1">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, which permanently eliminates the possibility of carrying a pregnancy. In the context of cervical cancer, this surgery typically includes removal of the cervix as well as the uterus, making it a treatment option for the disease. However, hysterectomy is only considered an appropriate treatment for cervical cancer when the cancer has not spread beyond the cervix; it is not performed if the cancer has metastasized to other areas.
Relationships:
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that removes more connective tissue and the upper vagina
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: EXTRAFASCIAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Extrafascial hysterectomy, also referred to as simple hysterectomy, is a surgical procedure and a specific type of hysterectomy. As a form of hysterectomy, it involves the surgical removal of the uterus. More precisely, the extrafascial approach removes only the uterus and the cervix. Thus, while it is categorized under the broader term "hysterectomy," it is distinguished by its limited scope of tissue removal, leaving other surrounding structures intact. This description incorporates both the general classification and the specific anatomical details of the procedure.
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: A hysterectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the uterus. Modified radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that, in addition to removing the uterus, also removes part of the surrounding connective tissue and a portion of the vagina. This distinguishes it from a standard hysterectomy, which typically removes only the uterus and possibly the cervix. The modified radical hysterectomy is thus a more extensive procedure than a simple hysterectomy, as it addresses additional tissue to achieve a wider margin of removal.
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: OVARY PRESERVATION
  - Description: The ovaries may or may not be removed during hysterectomy; ovary preservation keeps them intact
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Hysterectomy is a type of surgery. Specifically, it is a surgical procedure that removes the uterus. As a form of surgery, hysterectomy falls under the broader category of surgical procedures.
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Description: Hysterectomy is considered in cervical cancer treatment but is not performed if cancer has spread beyond the cervix.
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Hysterectomy is surgery to remove the uterus and cervix to treat cervical cancer
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION
  - Description: Pelvic lymph nodes may be removed during hysterectomy
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: SURGICAL MENOPAUSE
  - Description: Surgical menopause can be caused by hysterectomy that removes the ovaries
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: MENOPAUSE
  - Description: If hysterectomy removes both ovaries, it causes menopause
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING
  - Description: In cases where cervical cancer has spread beyond the cervix, a hysterectomy is not performed. Instead, pre-treatment surgical staging is considered to assess the extent of the disease. As part of this pre-treatment surgical staging, lymph nodes may be removed and tested to determine whether the cancer has spread to those nodes. This approach helps guide treatment decisions without proceeding to hysterectomy.
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: LYMPH NODE REMOVAL
  - Description: When cancer has spread beyond the cervix, a hysterectomy is not performed; instead, lymph nodes may be removed for staging.</data>
    </node>
