    <node id="MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING">
      <data key="d0">MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a type of medical imaging test that uses strong magnetic fields and radio waves—without exposing the patient to radiation—to produce detailed pictures of soft tissues in the body. It is particularly useful for examining structures such as the uterus, cervix, and vagina, and can help determine whether cancer has spread to nearby areas.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), also referred to simply as IMAGING in certain contexts, is a type of imaging that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed pictures of the body's internal structures. As an imaging test, MRI provides highly detailed images without the use of ionizing radiation, making it a valuable diagnostic tool.
  - Source: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Target: X-RAY
  - Description: MRI, unlike x-ray, does not use radiation.
  - Source: COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: CT and MRI are imaging methods used to evaluate cancer; MRI is an alternative in some cases and does not use radiation.
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: PET may be performed with MRI instead of CT in some cases.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
Description: Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a type of medical imaging test that uses strong magnetic fields and radio waves—without exposing the patient to radiation—to produce detailed pictures of soft tissues in the body. It is particularly useful for examining structures such as the uterus, cervix, and vagina, and can help determine whether cancer has spread to nearby areas.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), also referred to simply as IMAGING in certain contexts, is a type of imaging that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed pictures of the body's internal structures. As an imaging test, MRI provides highly detailed images without the use of ionizing radiation, making it a valuable diagnostic tool.
  - Source: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Target: X-RAY
  - Description: MRI, unlike x-ray, does not use radiation.
  - Source: COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: CT and MRI are imaging methods used to evaluate cancer; MRI is an alternative in some cases and does not use radiation.
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: PET may be performed with MRI instead of CT in some cases.

---</data>
    </node>
