ENTITY: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: High-dose chemotherapy is an intensive treatment step used in the context of stem cell rescue. It involves administering high doses of chemotherapy drugs with the dual purpose of destroying lymphoma cells and eradicating the remaining stem cells in the bone marrow. As a result of this aggressive treatment, the bone marrow's ability to produce blood cells is severely compromised, leading to low blood counts and a substantially increased vulnerability to infection. This step is typically the second phase of a stem cell rescue procedure, following initial collection or preparation, and is associated with significant side effects due to the high intensity of the chemotherapy agents used.
Relationships:
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: INFECTIONS
  - Description: The body is very vulnerable to infections during high-dose chemotherapy
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: High-dose chemotherapy is a more intensive form of standard chemotherapy.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: High-dose chemotherapy is an intensive form of chemotherapy
  - Source: STEM CELL RESCUE
  - Target: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure used in the treatment of lymphoma, and high-dose chemotherapy is a critical component of this process. High-dose chemotherapy is administered as a key step in stem cell rescue, specifically designed to kill lymphoma cells before the replacement of stem cells takes place. It serves as the second, cell-destroying step of the overall stem cell rescue procedure, highlighting its role in eradicating malignant cells to prepare the body for new, healthy stem cells. Together, these entities form a treatment strategy in which high-dose chemotherapy clears the way for stem cell replacement, making it an essential part of the rescue process.
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: LYMPHOMA
  - Description: High-dose chemotherapy is given to kill lymphoma cells in the body
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: FATIGUE
  - Description: High-dose chemotherapy makes patients feel very fatigued
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: STEM CELL REMOVAL
  - Description: Stem cells are removed and stored before high-dose chemotherapy is given during a stem cell rescue
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: STEM CELL REPLACEMENT
  - Description: High-dose chemotherapy is given after stem cell removal and before stem cell replacement to destroy remaining lymphoma cells
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: STEM CELL TRANSFUSION
  - Description: High-dose chemotherapy is given before the harvested stem cells are transfused back into the patient.
  - Source: STEM CELL HARVEST
  - Target: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Stem cell harvest occurs before high-dose chemotherapy in the stem cell rescue sequence.

ENTITY: TMB-H
Description: High tumor mutational burden; pembrolizumab, nivolumab, or ipilimumab might be used for TMB-H tumors in some cases.
Relationships:
  - Source: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Target: TMB-H
  - Description: Both TMB-H and MSI-H/dMMR are tumor mutation statuses that may guide treatment with immunotherapy drugs.
  - Source: TUMOR MUTATIONAL BURDEN (TMB)
  - Target: TMB-H
  - Description: TMB-H is defined as a tumor mutational burden of 10 or more mutations per million base pairs.

ENTITY: HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONAL
Description: Medical provider who can evaluate anal symptoms and perform procedures such as high-resolution anoscopy
Relationships:
  - Source: HIGH-RESOLUTION ANOSCOPY
  - Target: HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONAL
  - Description: Health care providers trained in high-resolution anoscopy conduct the procedure

ENTITY: INFECTIONS
Description: Infections are illnesses caused by pathogens, and they are commonly included in family history questions as part of medical assessments. In the context of cancer treatment, infections are also a frequent side effect of targeted therapy, and the body becomes particularly vulnerable to infections during high-dose chemotherapy. Thus, infections represent both a general category of disease and a specific clinical concern for patients undergoing certain cancer treatments.
Relationships:
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: INFECTIONS
  - Description: The body is very vulnerable to infections during high-dose chemotherapy
  - Source: TARGETED THERAPY
  - Target: INFECTIONS
  - Description: Infections are a common side effect of targeted therapy
  - Source: INFECTIONS
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Family history questions include illnesses, infections, and injuries.

ENTITY: HYPERTHERMIC INTRAPERITONEAL CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: A technique used during interval cytoreductive surgery for stage 3 ovarian cancer, in which warmed chemotherapy is circulated in the spaces between abdominal organs; abbreviated HIPEC
Relationships:
  - Source: INTERVAL CYTOREDUCTIVE SURGERY
  - Target: HYPERTHERMIC INTRAPERITONEAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: HIPEC may be used during interval cytoreductive surgery for stage 3 ovarian cancer

