    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER TO SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER TO SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER TO SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of cervical cancer. It arises from the squamous epithelial cells that line the outer surface of the cervix, and the vast majority of cervical cancers are of this subtype. Early detection through regular screening and HPV vaccination plays a key role in reducing the incidence and mortality of both squamous cell carcinoma and cervical cancer overall.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER to SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA
Description: Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of cervical cancer. It arises from the squamous epithelial cells that line the outer surface of the cervix, and the vast majority of cervical cancers are of this subtype. Early detection through regular screening and HPV vaccination plays a key role in reducing the incidence and mortality of both squamous cell carcinoma and cervical cancer overall.</data>
    </node>
