    <node id="METASTATIC CANCER">
      <data key="d0">METASTATIC CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">METASTATIC CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from its original site to distant organs, most commonly the liver, lungs, and/or bone. It may be classified as stage 4B if the spread is detected at the time of initial diagnosis, or as a distant recurrence if the cancer returns after prior treatment. For example, metastatic cervical cancer is specifically defined as stage 4B disease or a distant recurrence of cervical cancer. Treatment for metastatic cancer generally includes expanded biomarker testing to guide therapy, local therapies directed at liver and lung tumors, and systemic therapy, which circulates throughout the body to target cancer cells. In cases such as metastatic cervical cancer, the response to systemic therapy is monitored with imaging. Overall, metastatic cancer represents an advanced stage of disease requiring a comprehensive, multidisciplinary approach tailored to the individual patient's condition and cancer type.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 TESTING">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 TESTING</data>
      <data key="d1">PD-L1 TESTING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">PD-L1 testing is a biomarker test used to measure the expression of programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1) on cancer cells. It checks for the presence of PD-L1, a protein that cancer cells can express to evade immune detection, in order to guide the use of immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy. The test quantifies PD-L1 expression using a combined positive score (CPS), which assesses the number of PD-L1-positive cells relative to viable tumor cells. As a diagnostic tool, PD-L1 testing is designed to help activate the immune system to fight cancer by identifying patients who are most likely to benefit from immune checkpoint inhibitors, thereby informing treatment decisions.
Relationships:
  - Source: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Target: PD-L1 TESTING
  - Description: PD-L1 testing is a type of biomarker testing
  - Source: PD-L1 TESTING
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: A biopsy sample of the tumor may be tested for PD-L1 protein expression.
  - Source: PD-L1 TESTING
  - Target: IMMUNE CHECKPOINT INHIBITOR THERAPY
  - Description: PD-L1 testing indicates whether a patient might benefit from immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy combined with chemotherapy.
  - Source: PD-L1 TESTING
  - Target: COMBINED POSITIVE SCORE
  - Description: PD-L1 expression is measured using the combined positive score</data>
    </node>
