    <node id="CYSTOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d1">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Cystoscopy is a medical procedure that uses a cystoscope, a specialized viewing device, inserted through the urethra to examine the urethra, bladder, and urinary tract. It is commonly performed to look for signs of cancer, particularly in the bladder, and may also be used to diagnose or evaluate other urinary tract conditions. The procedure can be conducted in a urologist's office, a hospital, or a clinic, depending on the clinical setting and patient needs. While some descriptions note that cystoscopy may be performed under anesthesia—which may be required for certain diagnostic or therapeutic purposes—it is not always performed under general anesthesia; the level of sedation or anesthesia can vary based on the specific situation and the patient's condition. During cystoscopy, the physician can directly visualize the inside of the bladder and urethra, and the procedure allows for biopsy of suspicious tissue and the removal of very small tumors. Additionally, a pyelogram is usually performed during cystoscopy to provide imaging of the upper urinary tract, including the kidneys and ureters, enhancing the diagnostic capabilities of the procedure. Overall, cystoscopy is a versatile and important diagnostic and interventional tool for evaluating the urinary system, especially for detecting and managing bladder cancer and other abnormalities.
Relationships:
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: BLUE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Cystoscopy is a medical procedure that allows doctors to examine the inside of the bladder and urethra. Blue light cystoscopy is a newer, specialized type of cystoscopy that enhances this examination. It uses blue light and a photosensitizing dye, which causes abnormal tissue to appear differently under the blue light, aiding in the detection and evaluation of conditions such as bladder cancer. Together, cystoscopy and blue light cystoscopy represent standard and advanced approaches to urological diagnosis and treatment.
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging is used during cystoscopy to detect tumors more clearly
  - Source: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging can be used during cystoscopy to improve tumor detection.
  - Source: URINE CYTOLOGY
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: A urine sample can be collected during cystoscopy; if urine cytology finds abnormal cells, a cystoscopy is needed to locate cancer in the urethra or bladder
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: WHITE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: White light cystoscopy is the standard form of cystoscopy that uses white light
  - Source: WHITE LIGHT
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: White light is the standard light used during cystoscopy for tumor detection.
  - Source: EXAMINATIONS UNDER ANESTHESIA
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Cystoscopy is one of the examinations that can be performed under anesthesia
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: URETEROSCOPY
  - Description: Ureteroscopy is a very similar exam to cystoscopy but goes farther to examine the ureters and inner kidneys
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Biopsy can be done during cystoscopy using a small tool on the cystoscope to remove suspicious tissue; if not done then, it may be scheduled separately
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Cystoscopy is a medical procedure used to visually examine the interior of the bladder and urinary tract. In the context of cervical cancer, cystoscopy plays an important diagnostic role. Specifically, cystoscopy is used to look for signs of cancer within the bladder, and it may be needed to detect whether cervical cancer has spread to the bladder. Additionally, cystoscopy may be used to check both the bladder and the urinary tract for evidence of cervical cancer metastasis. Thus, for individuals with cervical cancer, cystoscopy helps determine whether the cancer has invaded nearby urinary structures, aiding in staging and treatment planning.
  - Source: BLADDER CANCER
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Cystoscopy looks for cancer inside the bladder
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are procedures used under anesthesia to examine organs for signs of cancer
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: If cystoscopy does not find cancer in the urethra or bladder, imaging tests are used to investigate the ureters, kidneys, or other areas
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: TESTING
  - Description: Cystoscopy is a testing procedure used to look for signs of cancer in the bladder
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: TUMOR REMOVAL
  - Description: If a tumor is found during cystoscopy, it must be removed in an operating room; very small growths can be removed during cystoscopy
  - Source: PYELOGRAM
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: A pyelogram is usually done during cystoscopy.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRACHYTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy in which a radioactive source is placed close to, in direct contact with, or inside the tumor or body. As an internal radiation therapy, it delivers a high radiation dose directly to the tumor while limiting exposure to surrounding healthy tissue. Brachytherapy is commonly used in the treatment of cervical cancer, including as an internal radiation treatment after surgery or for pelvic recurrence. For locally advanced cervical cancer, it is recommended in combination with chemoradiation.
Relationships:
  - Source: EBRT
  - Target: BRACHYTHERAPY
  - Description: Brachytherapy may be given along with EBRT in the treatment of locally advanced cervical cancer

---</data>
    </node>
