      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: SURVEILLANCE
Description: SURVEILLANCE is the regular, ongoing follow-up monitoring after cancer treatment, designed to detect early signs that the cancer may have returned (recurrence). It begins when there are no signs of cancer after initial treatment, and also continues after treatment for a recurrence. Surveillance care includes physical exams, pelvic exams, and a range of tests and imaging tailored to the type of cancer. For colon cancer, surveillance typically involves colonoscopy, physical exams, carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) blood tests, and CT scans. For anal canal or perianal cancer, surveillance includes imaging, lymph node exams, digital rectal exams, and anoscopy. In specific settings, such as after endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) or transanal local excision, surveillance is recommended for at least five years and may include flexible sigmoidoscopy, proctoscopy, endorectal ultrasound, pelvic MRI, and colonoscopy. Overall, surveillance is the follow-up care process described in cancer treatment documents as essential for catching recurrence as early as possible, thereby improving outcomes.
Relationships:
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: RECURRENCE
  - Description: Surveillance is performed to detect cancer recurrence or new cancer growth early.
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Surveillance is conducted to monitor for recurrence or relapse.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Surveillance is part of the post-treatment management for colon cancer.
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Surveillance is performed to find early signs that anal cancer may have returned
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: Surveillance and imaging are closely intertwined in the management of anal cancer. Imaging is a recommended component of surveillance for patients with stage II and III anal cancers who have achieved complete remission. As part of post-treatment follow-up, imaging is also recommended every year for three years during surveillance after treatment for recurrence. Additionally, imaging is employed during surveillance when new or worsening symptoms arise that suggest possible recurrence or spread of disease. The surveillance process specifically includes imaging modalities such as endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI to monitor for disease status. In summary, imaging serves as a routine, scheduled tool in surveillance protocols, as well as a targeted diagnostic measure when clinical signs raise concern, with the choice of modality typically involving endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI.
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Surveillance is recommended after completing chemoradiation to check treatment results
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: CT SCAN
  - Description: CT scans are recommended as part of surveillance for stage 2 and 3 colon cancer.
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Description: Physical exams are part of surveillance for stage 2 and 3 colon cancer.
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM
  - Description: DREs every 3 to 6 months for 5 years are part of surveillance after lymph node-only treatment
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: IMAGING TEST
  - Description: Imaging tests may be ordered if recurrence or spread is suspected and are used in surveillance when indicated.
  - Source: TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Surveillance is recommended for 5 years after transanal local excision to check for recurrence
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Surveillance and anoscopy are closely related in post-treatment monitoring. Anoscopy is a recommended component of surveillance protocols when feasible. Specifically, after lymph node-only treatment, surveillance includes anoscopy performed every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years. Thus, the surveillance strategy incorporates regular anoscopic examinations to monitor for potential recurrence or complications, with the frequency and duration clearly defined in the context of lymph node-only treatment.
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is recommended during surveillance after ESD and after transanal local excision
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is a surveillance examination performed after transanal local excision
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Description: A complete remission result leads to the recommended surveillance schedule described in the text
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: COLONOSCOPY
  - Description: Colonoscopy is part of surveillance after transanal local excision, with intervals based on prior results
  - Source: COLONOSCOPY
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Colonoscopy is a surveillance test recommended at scheduled intervals after colon cancer treatment.
  - Source: TUMOR REMOVAL
