    <node id="ANOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">ANOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d1">ANOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">ANOSCOPY is a medical procedure used to inspect the anal canal and lower rectum using an anoscope. It is typically performed as a surveillance exam during follow-up care for individuals treated for anal cancer, including during remission. The procedure is recommended every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years after treatment, when feasible. During follow-up, anoscopy is often combined with a digital rectal examination to ensure thorough assessment. In cases where treatment was limited to lymph nodes only, anoscopy is still recommended on the same schedule, every 6 to 12 months for 3 years; however, it may be omitted if the procedure causes significant pain, particularly after radiation therapy.
Relationships:
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Surveillance and anoscopy are closely related in post-treatment monitoring. Anoscopy is a recommended component of surveillance protocols when feasible. Specifically, after lymph node-only treatment, surveillance includes anoscopy performed every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years. Thus, the surveillance strategy incorporates regular anoscopic examinations to monitor for potential recurrence or complications, with the frequency and duration clearly defined in the context of lymph node-only treatment.
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: After radiation therapy, anoscopy may be impossible due to pain and can be omitted or performed less often
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: The physical exam is performed together with anoscopy.
  - Source: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Both DREs and anoscopy are follow-up tests recommended after treatment for cancer in lymph nodes only
  - Source: FOLLOW-UP AND SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Follow-up and surveillance includes anoscopy.
  - Source: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Digital rectal examination and anoscopy are recommended together during the follow-up exam.
  - Source: DRE
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Surveillance during remission includes DRE and anoscopy on different schedules
  - Source: ANOSCOPY
  - Target: LYMPH NODE EXAMS
  - Description: Both are surveillance exams performed during remission after anal cancer treatment</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND">
      <data key="d0">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d1">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Endorectal ultrasound, also known as EUS, is a diagnostic imaging test that uses a small probe inserted into the rectum. The probe emits sound waves to create detailed images of the pelvis, allowing physicians to determine the extent of cancer in the area. It can also be used to guide biopsy procedures. In addition to its role in initial staging and diagnosis, endorectal ultrasound serves as an important surveillance tool after treatments such as endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) and transanal local excision. For patients undergoing surveillance, the test is recommended every 3 to 6 months for the first two years, and then every 6 months through the fifth year following treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: EUS may be used to guide a biopsy of lymph nodes or other areas near the rectum
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is recommended during surveillance after ESD and after transanal local excision
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is an imaging option in surveillance after transanal local excision
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: Imaging may be performed using endorectal ultrasound
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
  - Target: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is an imaging test that can show the extent of cancer in the pelvis
  - Source: MRI
  - Target: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound may be done instead of pelvic MRI when MRI cannot be used or its images are unhelpful
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: PELVIC MRI
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound and pelvic MRI with contrast are alternative imaging examinations recommended during surveillance
  - Source: ESD
  - Target: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is recommended at intervals during surveillance after ESD</data>
    </node>
