    <node id="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS">
      <data key="d0">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
      <data key="d1">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary condition and an inherited syndrome that significantly increases the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. It is also closely related to colon cancer, as it is an inherited syndrome that causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum. These polyps can eventually lead to invasive colon cancer if left untreated. Thus, FAP is a genetic disorder characterized by the development of numerous colorectal polyps and an elevated risk for both adrenal tumors and invasive colorectal cancer.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the disease's onset.</data>
