    <node id="FOLFOX">
      <data key="d0">FOLFOX</data>
      <data key="d1">FOLFOX</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen that combines three drugs: 5-fluorouracil (5-FU), leucovorin, and oxaliplatin. It is specifically recommended for the treatment of high-risk stage 2 and stage 3 colon cancers. The standard course of FOLFOX therapy is administered over a period of 6 months. This regimen is a common and effective option in adjuvant chemotherapy for these colon cancer stages, aiming to reduce the risk of recurrence after surgery.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: FOLFOX
  - Description: FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen used in the treatment of certain cancers. As part of the broader category of chemotherapy, FOLFOX combines multiple anti-cancer drugs and is administered intravenously. It is specifically indicated as a treatment option for metastatic anal cancer, offering a systemic approach for patients with advanced disease. Additionally, FOLFOX is recommended as a chemotherapy regimen for high-risk stage 2 and stage 3 colon cancers, where it is used to reduce the risk of recurrence after surgical resection. Thus, FOLFOX serves as a versatile chemotherapy protocol in oncology, applied across different gastrointestinal malignancies depending on disease stage and clinical context.
  - Source: METASTASIS
  - Target: FOLFOX
  - Description: FOLFOX is used to treat metastatic anal cancer
  - Source: FOLFOX
  - Target: CAPEOX
  - Description: FOLFOX and CAPEOX are both recommended as preferred chemotherapy regimens for high-risk colon cancers.</data>
    </node>
