    <node id="APL">
      <data key="d0">APL</data>
      <data key="d1">APL</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">APL (acute promyelocytic leukemia) is a rare subtype of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). It is characterized by the abnormal fusion gene PML::RARA, which results from a translocation between chromosomes 15 and 17. This genetic abnormality is a defining feature of the disease and drives its clinical behavior. APL is discussed in Chapter 5 of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which provides information about this specific leukemia subtype. As a distinct form of AML, APL requires specialized diagnostic evaluation and management approaches due to its unique genetic basis and clinical characteristics.
Relationships:
  - Source: APL
  - Target: AML
  - Description: APL is a rare subtype of AML.
  - Source: AML
  - Target: APL
  - Description: APL is a rare subtype of AML.
  - Source: APL
  - Target: BPDCN
  - Description: APL (acute promyelocytic leukemia) and BPDCN (blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm) are both recognized as subtypes of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These two entities are addressed together in the same NCCN patient guidelines, which provide comprehensive guidance for patients and caregivers on these specific AML subtypes.
  - Source: NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK
  - Target: APL
  - Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides patient-focused guidance that covers acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients include a dedicated chapter on the treatment of APL. This chapter is part of the organization's broader patient education materials, which translate the clinical NCCN Guidelines into accessible information for individuals affected by cancer. The inclusion of APL treatment in the NCCN patient guidelines reflects the network's commitment to helping patients understand standard care options for this specific leukemia subtype. Both descriptions affirm the same fact: the NCCN’s patient guide contains a treatment-focused chapter on APL.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
