    <node id="NARROW-BAND IMAGING">
      <data key="d0">NARROW-BAND IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">NARROW-BAND IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">NARROW-BAND IMAGING is a relatively new imaging technique used during cystoscopy to improve the detection of bladder tumors. Unlike standard white-light imaging, this method enhances the visibility of tumors without the need for any dye, allowing abnormal tissue to be identified more clearly. Because it is still in the testing phase, its use is currently considered investigational, but it shows promise for detecting bladder tumors better than conventional approaches.
Relationships:
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging is used during cystoscopy to detect tumors more clearly
  - Source: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging can be used during cystoscopy to improve tumor detection.
  - Source: BLUE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Description: Both are newer detection techniques compared with white light cystoscopy and are still in the testing phase
  - Source: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Target: BLUE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are relatively new imaging methods being tested for bladder tumor detection.
  - Source: WHITE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging may detect tumors better than standard white light cystoscopy
  - Source: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Target: WHITE LIGHT
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging is a newer method that may detect tumors better than standard white light detection.</data>
