    <node id="RADICAL CYSTECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">RADICAL CYSTECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d1">RADICAL CYSTECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Radical cystectomy is the most widely used surgical treatment for muscle-invasive bladder cancer. It is a major operation that involves the removal of the entire bladder, nearby lymph nodes, and sometimes other organs in the pelvis. Because it is a significant procedure, it requires a hospital stay and a recovery period that typically lasts from two to three months or longer.
Relationships:
  - Source: CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is a type of cystectomy that removes the entire bladder along with nearby lymph nodes and possibly other organs.
  - Source: TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF BLADDER TUMOR (TURBT)
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: Both TURBT and radical cystectomy are surgical treatment options for bladder cancer; TURBT removes tumors from the bladder wall and radical cystectomy removes the bladder.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: NCCN's patient education chapter describes radical cystectomy as the most common surgery for muscle-invasive bladder cancer.
  - Source: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: BLADDER REMOVAL SURGERY
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is the most widely used surgery for muscle-invasive bladder cancer and is a form of bladder removal surgery.
  - Source: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: URINARY DIVERSION
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the bladder. Urinary diversion is a complementary surgical procedure performed at the same time as radical cystectomy, designed to provide a new way for urine to leave the body. Together, these procedures ensure that, after the bladder is removed, a functional pathway for urine excretion is established. Urinary diversion is thus an integral part of the overall surgical management when radical cystectomy is performed.</data>
