    <node id="POLYPECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">POLYPECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d1">POLYPECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps. This procedure is used to extract abnormal growths, known as polyps, from the lining of the colon or rectum. By removing these polyps during the endoscopic examination, polypectomy helps prevent potential complications, such as the development of colorectal cancer, and allows for pathological analysis of the removed tissue. The procedure is typically conducted by a gastroenterologist or surgeon using specialized instruments passed through the colonoscope, and it is considered a routine and minimally invasive intervention. Overall, polypectomy is an essential component of colon cancer screening and prevention, enabling both diagnosis and treatment in a single session.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLONOSCOPY
  - Target: POLYPECTOMY
  - Description: Colonoscopy and polypectomy are directly connected medical procedures, as polypectomy is performed during a colonoscopy. A colonoscopy is a diagnostic and therapeutic procedure that allows a physician to visually examine the interior of the colon. When polyps are detected during this examination, they are typically removed right away using a polypectomy. Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure specifically designed to remove polyps from the lining of the colon. Most polyps can be successfully removed in this manner during a colonoscopy, making polypectomy the standard method for polyp removal. Thus, the two procedures are integrally linked, with colonoscopy providing the access and visualization needed for the polypectomy to be performed safely and effectively.
  - Source: POLYPECTOMY
  - Target: COLECTOMY
  - Description: If a polypectomy is not sufficient, surgery/resection of a larger part of the colon (colectomy) may be needed.
  - Source: POLYPECTOMY
  - Target: RESECTION
  - Description: A resection may be needed after a polypectomy depending on polyp size, shape, polypectomy results, and tissue test results.
  - Source: POLYPS
  - Target: POLYPECTOMY
  - Description: Polyps can be removed by polypectomy during colonoscopy
  - Source: POLYPECTOMY
  - Target: PELVIC MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: Pelvic MRI is recommended for some polyps, especially sessile polyps, to help guide decisions about surgery after polypectomy.
  - Source: POLYPECTOMY
  - Target: ENDOSCOPIC ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Endoscopic ultrasound is recommended for some polyps to help guide decisions about surgery after treatment, including after polypectomy.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
