    <node id="CONFIRMATION OF DIAGNOSIS DETERMINES TREATMENT APPROACH">
      <data key="d0">CONFIRMATION OF DIAGNOSIS DETERMINES TREATMENT APPROACH</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFIRMATION OF DIAGNOSIS DETERMINES TREATMENT APPROACH</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">: Once primary CNS lymphoma is confirmed, the care team discusses treatment options, which may include chemotherapy, radiation therapy, stem cell transplant, targeted therapy, immunotherapy, or a combination of these, as well as participation in a clinical trial [Data: Sources (187); Entities (2697, 2699, 2701, 2750, 2760); Relationships (2697, 2699, 2701, 2750, 2760)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTENT OF DISEASE INFLUENCES TREATMENT PHASES">
      <data key="d0">EXTENT OF DISEASE INFLUENCES TREATMENT PHASES</data>
      <data key="d1">EXTENT OF DISEASE INFLUENCES TREATMENT PHASES</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">: The diagnostic tests determine whether cancer is present in the spine or spinal fluid, which may lead to a different induction therapy being recommended [Data: Entities (1113); Relationships (2816); Sources (195)]. Similarly, if lymphoma involves the eyes, treatment may be expanded to include the eyes through WBRT or focused radiation therapy [Data: Entities (1080, 1108); Relationships (2740, 2745); Sources (193)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERFORMANCE STATUS AND OVERALL HEALTH GUIDE TREATMENT INTENSITY">
      <data key="d0">PERFORMANCE STATUS AND OVERALL HEALTH GUIDE TREATMENT INTENSITY</data>
      <data key="d1">PERFORMANCE STATUS AND OVERALL HEALTH GUIDE TREATMENT INTENSITY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">: The evaluation of the patient's ability to perform daily activities helps determine the level of treatment they can handle and whether they may be eligible for a clinical trial [Data: Sources (188)]. This is particularly relevant for intensive treatments like high-dose chemotherapy with stem cell rescue, which is not appropriate for everyone [Data: Sources (193)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TREATMENT IS ORGANIZED INTO PHASES">
      <data key="d0">TREATMENT IS ORGANIZED INTO PHASES</data>
      <data key="d1">TREATMENT IS ORGANIZED INTO PHASES</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">: Based on the diagnostic findings, treatment typically follows a two-step process: the **induction phase**, which aims to eliminate as many lymphoma cells as possible (often using high-dose methotrexate chemotherapy combined with rituximab), followed by the **consolidation phase**, which aims to destroy any remaining cancer cells and prevent relapse [Data: Entities (1111, 1112); Relationships (2815); Sources (195)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROGNOSTIC CONSIDERATIONS">
      <data key="d0">PROGNOSTIC CONSIDERATIONS</data>
      <data key="d1">PROGNOSTIC CONSIDERATIONS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">: The diagnostic process also informs discussions about prognosis. While cure is rare, it may be possible if the disease is diagnosed early and treated immediately, particularly in younger, healthier patients. Even when cure is not possible, treatment can slow growth, reduce symptoms, and prolong life [Data: Entities (1071); Relationships (2725, 2717); Sources (187)].

---
</data>
    </node>
