    <node id="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Intravesical BCG therapy is the bladder cancer treatment in which BCG is placed into the bladder, usually after TURBT; side effects and maintenance schedules are discussed.
Relationships:
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a form of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: TURBT
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is scheduled after TURBT and at intervals after the procedure.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy may be used instead of intravesical BCG if BCG is unavailable or ineffective, and both may cause similar side effects.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: MAINTENANCE THERAPY
  - Description: Maintenance therapy may be continued after intravesical BCG therapy every 6 months for up to 3 years.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: If intravesical BCG doesn't work on its own, other treatment options are available in addition to treatment in clinical trials.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: BCG SHORTAGE
  - Description: Because of the BCG shortage, many patients cannot receive intravesical BCG therapy or receive a reduced dose.
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a type of BCG therapy given directly into the bladder.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: BCG MAINTENANCE THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy may be followed by BCG maintenance therapy.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Intravesical chemotherapy is chemotherapy given directly into the bladder after TURBT or as an alternative when BCG is unavailable or ineffective, to treat cancer and reduce recurrence.
Relationships:
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy may be used instead of intravesical BCG if BCG is unavailable or ineffective, and both may cause similar side effects.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy is a type of intravesical therapy.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCG THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">BCG therapy is a bladder cancer treatment that has been used for decades, but it has been in short supply in the United States and worldwide; it can be given intravesically and may be reduced or shortened during shortages.
Relationships:
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a type of BCG therapy given directly into the bladder.</data>
    </node>
