ENTITY: MUTATION
Description: Mutation is a change or error in the genetic code of a cell, specifically an abnormal alteration in genes or chromosomes. These changes can be inherited from one’s parents or acquired during a person’s lifetime, and they may increase the risk of developing cancer. Mutations are the underlying starting point for many cancers, including bladder cancer and acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where they are often unique to the cancer cells themselves. In the context of AML, mutation testing is performed using techniques such as karyotype analysis, fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH), next-generation sequencing (NGS), and polymerase chain reaction (PCR). These tests identify specific genetic abnormalities that can guide treatment decisions. Importantly, some mutations can be directly targeted with specific therapies, making mutation profiling a critical component of personalized cancer care. Overall, a mutation represents a fundamental error or change in the genetic material that can drive cancer development and, when identified, offers opportunities for tailored treatment strategies.
Relationships:
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation is a type of mutation
  - Source: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Biomarker testing identifies the presence or absence of mutations
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: GENETIC TESTING
  - Description: Genetic testing identifies mutations in genes
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Genetic testing is done to look for inherited and tumor-related mutations
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR
  - Description: Deficient mismatch repair arises when DNA mutations prevent mismatch repair proteins from fixing errors.
  - Source: CANCER
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Cancer begins with mutations in genes. These genetic mutations can cause cancer by disrupting normal cell regulation. Tumor tests can identify specific mutations, and those mutations can then be targeted with specific therapies.
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: PATIENT
  - Description: The patient may carry inherited or acquired mutations relevant to cancer risk.
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: BLADDER CANCER
  - Description: Bladder cancer starts with mutations in genes
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: A genetic test is used to find abnormal changes (mutations) in genes.
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: AML
  - Description: Mutations are unique to AML cells and detected by mutation testing
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Description: A germline mutation is an inherited type of mutation.
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Germline mutation is a type of mutation that is inherited and present in every cell
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Tumor testing looks for specific abnormalities and mutations in DNA repair genes
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: SOMATIC MUTATION
  - Description: A somatic mutation is an acquired type of mutation.
  - Source: SOMATIC MUTATION
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Somatic mutation is a type of mutation acquired later in life in some cells
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: TUMOR MUTATION TESTING
  - Description: Tumor mutation testing detects DNA mutations in tumor samples.
  - Source: CHROMOSOME TRANSLOCATION
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: A chromosome translocation is a chromosomal mutation or abnormality
  - Source: GENE REARRANGEMENT
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: A gene rearrangement is a genetic mutation or abnormality
  - Source: LEUKEMIA PREDISPOSITION SYNDROMES
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Predisposition syndromes are linked to inherited genetic mutations that raise leukemia risk
  - Source: HEREDITARY GENE TESTING
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Hereditary gene testing looks for inherited mutations in cancer risk genes
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: TUMOR MUTATIONAL BURDEN-HIGH
  - Description: TMB-H is defined by a high number of mutations per million base pairs of tumor DNA.
  - Source: GERMLINE TESTING
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Germline testing looks for inherited germline mutations in genes.

ENTITY: LYNCH SYNDROME
Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes. It is a hereditary disorder that significantly increases the risk of developing several types of cancer, most notably colon cancer, as well as uterine (endometrial), ovarian, and other cancers. Lynch syndrome can also cause hereditary ovarian cancer and may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. Because of its strong hereditary nature and elevated cancer risks, Lynch syndrome is recognized as a factor that may make germline genetic testing appropriate for affected individuals and their families.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the disease’s onset.
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Genetic testing can identify Lynch syndrome and other hereditary syndromes
  - Source: OVARIAN CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is a cause of ovarian cancer in addition to colon and uterine cancers
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: GERMLINE TESTING
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is listed as a factor making germline testing more appropriate
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor.
  - Source: HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome can cause hereditary ovarian cancer
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: If colon cancer is dMMR/MSI-H, mismatch repair testing leads to testing for Lynch syndrome
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: GENETIC COUNSELING
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is a factor that should lead a person to ask about genetic counseling

ENTITY: RECTAL CANCER
Description: Rectal cancer is a malignant disease that forms in the rectum, which is the final section of the large intestine. As a cancer of the rectum, it is the focus of dedicated patient education materials: the NCCN Guidelines for Patients specifically address this disease and its treatment. These guidelines provide evidence-based information on managing rectal cancer, covering topics such as diagnosis, treatment options, and supportive care. The staging of rectal cancer ranges from stage I to stage III, and this classification is determined by two key factors: the extent of tumor growth into the rectal wall and the involvement of nearby lymph nodes. In summary, rectal cancer is a distinct cancer type with established clinical guidance for patients, and its staging system—based on local invasion and lymphatic spread—helps define the appropriate treatment approach.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLORECTAL CANCER
  - Target: RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer is a colorectal cancer that forms in the rectum
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: NCCN, the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, publishes patient-focused resources on rectal cancer. Specifically, NCCN makes available the NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Rectal Cancer, which are designed to provide patients with reliable, evidence-based information about the disease and its management. These guidelines translate the clinical NCCN Guidelines into plain language so that patients and caregivers can better understand rectal cancer treatment options, decision-making, and care planning. The publication is part of NCCN's broader mission to improve the quality and effectiveness of cancer care by ensuring patients have access to clear, expert-derived guidance. In summary, the entity NCCN is the publisher, and its patient guideline dedicated to rectal cancer is a key resource available to affected individuals and their families.
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens are used to treat rectal cancer
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Chemoradiation is a possible next treatment for stage 2 and 3 rectal cancers
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: SURGICAL STAGING
  - Description: Surgical staging determines how far the rectal cancer has grown and whether lymph nodes contain cancer
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: PATHOLOGIST
  - Description: A pathologist assesses how far the rectal cancer has grown and whether removed lymph nodes contain cancer
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION
  - Target: RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: APR is used for tumors in the lower rectum caused by rectal cancer
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: LABORATORY TESTING
  - Description: Laboratory testing detects cancer in tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: STAGE 2 RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer can be assigned stage 2 when the tumor has grown through the rectal wall
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: STAGE 3 RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer can be assigned stage 3 when cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes
  - Source: RECTAL CANCER
  - Target: STAGE 1 RECTAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal cancer can be assigned stage 1 when the tumor has grown into the second or third muscle layer of the rectal wall

