      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">Fever is recognized as a B symptom of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), where a patient experiences fever without any underlying infection or other illness [Data: Entities (240); Relationships (473)]. This is a disease-related symptom rather than an infectious cause.

**2. Drug-Induced Etiology:**
Fever is a common side effect of targeted therapy, which is used as a cancer treatment [Data: Entities (240); Relationships (2752)]. This represents an adverse reaction to medication rather than an infectious process.

**3. Infectious Etiology:**
Infections are illnesses caused by pathogens, and they are commonly included in family history questions as part of medical assessments [Data: Entities (243); Relationships (477)]. In the context of cancer treatment, infections are also a frequent side effect of targeted therapy, and the body becomes particularly vulnerable to infections during high-dose chemotherapy [Data: Entities (243); Relationships (2756, 2783)].

**4. Inflammatory Etiology:**
Inflammation is a bodily process that can appear as a bright spot on PET scans [Data: Entities (464); Relationships (920)]. Infection is a condition that the body fights using white blood cells and immune cells located in the lymph nodes, and it can also appear as a bright spot on PET scans [Data: Entities (465); Relationships (921)].
</data>
    </node>
