    <node id="LOCAL EXCISION">
      <data key="d0">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d1">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Local excision is a surgical procedure that removes only the tumor and a small area of surrounding tissue. It is a recommended treatment option for some early-stage, low-risk perianal cancers, particularly slow-growing stage I and II perianal cancers. In certain cases, local excision may be used as an alternative to chemoradiation for some anal cancers. This approach offers a more limited resection than broader surgeries, making it suitable for select patients whose tumors meet specific criteria.
Relationships:
  - Source: LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Chemoradiation is recommended for nonmetastatic perianal cancers that do not meet the criteria for local excision.
  - Source: LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: Radiation therapy, with or without chemotherapy, is an option when additional treatment is needed after local excision.
  - Source: LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Local excision is a surgical procedure, also referred to as surgery.
  - Source: LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Cancer staging determines whether local excision is an option, since it is recommended only for certain stage I and IIA cancers.
  - Source: LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: RE-EXCISION
  - Description: Re-excision is the preferred next treatment if local excision leaves a margin of less than 1 cm or a margin containing cancer cells.
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
  - Target: LOCAL EXCISION
  - Description: Local excision may be used as an alternative to chemoradiation for some anal cancers. Specifically, it can be considered instead of chemoradiation for certain slow-growing stage I and II perianal cancers. Thus, for select cases, local excision serves as a treatment option in place of chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
