    <node id="SKIN BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">SKIN BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">SKIN BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Skin biopsy is a diagnostic procedure in which a lesion or a small sample of skin tissue is removed and sent to a laboratory for analysis. It is the only way to confirm squamous cell skin cancer and is an essential part of testing for this condition. A pathologist examines the removed tissue to determine whether cancer is present. Skin biopsy can also involve taking a small sample of inflamed skin on the breast, which is often used to help diagnose inflammatory breast cancer. In all cases, the procedure is performed to remove a sample of skin and test it for cancer or other abnormal changes, with the results guiding further diagnosis and treatment.
**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: SKIN BIOPSY
Description: Skin biopsy is a diagnostic procedure in which a lesion or a small sample of skin tissue is removed and sent to a laboratory for analysis. It is the only way to confirm squamous cell skin cancer and is an essential part of testing for this condition. A pathologist examines the removed tissue to determine whether cancer is present. Skin biopsy can also involve taking a small sample of inflamed skin on the breast, which is often used to help diagnose inflammatory breast cancer. In all cases, the procedure is performed to remove a sample of skin and test it for cancer or other abnormal changes, with the results guiding further diagnosis and treatment.
**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">CORE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">CORE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Core biopsy (CB) is a medical procedure and a specific type of biopsy. It involves the use of a needle—typically a hollow needle—to remove a sample of tissue or fluid from the body. In particular, core biopsy is commonly employed to obtain tissue samples from a tumor, allowing for diagnostic examination. The procedure is minimally invasive and is used to gather material for further analysis, such as determining whether a tumor is benign or malignant.
**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CORE BIOPSY
Description: Core biopsy (CB) is a medical procedure and a specific type of biopsy. It involves the use of a needle—typically a hollow needle—to remove a sample of tissue or fluid from the body. In particular, core biopsy is commonly employed to obtain tissue samples from a tumor, allowing for diagnostic examination. The procedure is minimally invasive and is used to gather material for further analysis, such as determining whether a tumor is benign or malignant.
**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEEDLE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">NEEDLE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">NEEDLE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Needle biopsy is a medical procedure in which a needle is used to extract a sample of tissue or fluid from the body for diagnostic testing. It is often performed on a lymph node, allowing pathologists to examine the sample for abnormalities such as infection or cancer. The procedure is also known by another name: fine-needle aspiration (FNA), reflecting its minimally invasive nature and use of a thin needle to collect cellular material. When required, a needle biopsy may be carried out by a breast diagnostic radiologist, particularly in cases involving suspicious breast findings. In this context, the radiologist uses imaging guidance to precisely place the needle and obtain an adequate sample for evaluation. Thus, needle biopsy is a versatile and commonly used diagnostic tool that can be performed on various body sites, with a lymph node being one example, and is closely associated with fine-needle aspiration and radiological expertise.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: NEEDLE BIOPSY
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Needle biopsy is a form of biopsy
  - Source: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
  - Target: NEEDLE BIOPSY
  - Description: A lymph node biopsy is performed using a needle biopsy to take a sample from the lymph node</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: NEEDLE BIOPSY
Description: Needle biopsy is a medical procedure in which a needle is used to extract a sample of tissue or fluid from the body for diagnostic testing. It is often performed on a lymph node, allowing pathologists to examine the sample for abnormalities such as infection or cancer. The procedure is also known by another name: fine-needle aspiration (FNA), reflecting its minimally invasive nature and use of a thin needle to collect cellular material. When required, a needle biopsy may be carried out by a breast diagnostic radiologist, particularly in cases involving suspicious breast findings. In this context, the radiologist uses imaging guidance to precisely place the needle and obtain an adequate sample for evaluation. Thus, needle biopsy is a versatile and commonly used diagnostic tool that can be performed on various body sites, with a lymph node being one example, and is closely associated with fine-needle aspiration and radiological expertise.
