    <node id="CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Core needle biopsy (CNB) is a minimally invasive diagnostic procedure that uses a hollow core needle to remove tissue samples for examination. It is a type of needle-based biopsy specifically designed to extract samples from tumors or lymph nodes. The procedure is often performed under light sedation by an interventional radiologist or a surgeon. In the context of breast tissue, CNB can remove more than one tissue sample through the same area, making it a valuable method for evaluating suspicious lesions. Additionally, core needle biopsy is used to assess suspicious lymph nodes in cases of squamous cell skin cancer. Overall, CNB provides a reliable way to obtain tissue for pathological analysis while minimizing trauma compared to open surgical biopsy.
**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY
