    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → SURGERY">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Colectomy is a surgical procedure performed to treat colon cancer by removing cancerous tissue from the colon. As a colon cancer surgery, it serves as the primary intervention for excising malignant growths. Colectomy can be performed in several ways: as a one-stage procedure, in which the diseased portion is removed and the colon is immediately reconnected; with a colostomy, where the end of the intestine is brought through the abdominal wall to divert waste; or after stenting, a technique used to open a blocked colon prior to surgery, allowing for a safer or delayed operation. Following the colectomy, critical medical decisions regarding further treatment and surveillance recommendations are made. These post-surgical choices are based on the pathology results and the extent of cancer removal. Thus, colectomy not only removes the cancer but also serves as a pivotal point for determining the patient's ongoing care plan, including whether additional therapies such as chemotherapy or radiation are needed, and how frequently the patient should be monitored for recurrence. In summary, colectomy is both a curative surgical intervention and a foundational step in the management of colon cancer, guiding subsequent clinical decisions and long-term follow-up strategies.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → SURGERY
Description: Colectomy is a surgical procedure performed to treat colon cancer by removing cancerous tissue from the colon. As a colon cancer surgery, it serves as the primary intervention for excising malignant growths. Colectomy can be performed in several ways: as a one-stage procedure, in which the diseased portion is removed and the colon is immediately reconnected; with a colostomy, where the end of the intestine is brought through the abdominal wall to divert waste; or after stenting, a technique used to open a blocked colon prior to surgery, allowing for a safer or delayed operation. Following the colectomy, critical medical decisions regarding further treatment and surveillance recommendations are made. These post-surgical choices are based on the pathology results and the extent of cancer removal. Thus, colectomy not only removes the cancer but also serves as a pivotal point for determining the patient's ongoing care plan, including whether additional therapies such as chemotherapy or radiation are needed, and how frequently the patient should be monitored for recurrence. In summary, colectomy is both a curative surgical intervention and a foundational step in the management of colon cancer, guiding subsequent clinical decisions and long-term follow-up strategies.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER → PROCTOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER → PROCTOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER → PROCTOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Proctoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the anus and rectum, and it plays a role in evaluating certain cancers. Specifically, proctoscopy is used to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In the context of cervical cancer, proctoscopy may be needed to look for signs of cervical cancer in the bowel. Additionally, proctoscopy may be used to check the anus and rectum for cervical cancer spread, helping to determine whether the cancer has metastasized to these areas. Thus, for cervical cancer patients, proctoscopy serves as a diagnostic tool to assess possible bowel involvement and local spread of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: CERVICAL CANCER → PROCTOSCOPY
Description: Proctoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the anus and rectum, and it plays a role in evaluating certain cancers. Specifically, proctoscopy is used to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In the context of cervical cancer, proctoscopy may be needed to look for signs of cervical cancer in the bowel. Additionally, proctoscopy may be used to check the anus and rectum for cervical cancer spread, helping to determine whether the cancer has metastasized to these areas. Thus, for cervical cancer patients, proctoscopy serves as a diagnostic tool to assess possible bowel involvement and local spread of the disease.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part or all of the colon, often performed to treat colon cancer. Chemotherapy is a systemic treatment that uses drugs to destroy cancer cells. In the context of colon cancer treatment, chemotherapy is commonly administered after a colectomy for certain stages of the disease. It is not recommended after colectomy for stage 1 cancers, as the risk of recurrence is low and the benefits do not outweigh the potential side effects. For other, more advanced stages, chemotherapy may be given following surgery to eliminate any remaining cancer cells and reduce the risk of recurrence. Thus, while postoperative chemotherapy is a standard option for appropriate stages, its use is avoided in stage 1 cases.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part or all of the colon, often performed to treat colon cancer. Chemotherapy is a systemic treatment that uses drugs to destroy cancer cells. In the context of colon cancer treatment, chemotherapy is commonly administered after a colectomy for certain stages of the disease. It is not recommended after colectomy for stage 1 cancers, as the risk of recurrence is low and the benefits do not outweigh the potential side effects. For other, more advanced stages, chemotherapy may be given following surgery to eliminate any remaining cancer cells and reduce the risk of recurrence. Thus, while postoperative chemotherapy is a standard option for appropriate stages, its use is avoided in stage 1 cases.</data>
    </node>
