    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → COLOSTOMY">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → COLOSTOMY</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → COLOSTOMY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A colectomy is the surgical removal of all or part of the colon. A colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening (stoma) from the colon to the outside of the body, allowing stool to be diverted into a collection bag. A colostomy may be performed during a colectomy when it is not safe to reconnect the two healthy ends of the remaining colon. This situation can arise after cancerous tissue is removed, if the remaining sections cannot be safely rejoined. Additionally, a colectomy with colostomy is an option for treating a bowel blockage. Therefore, the combination of these two procedures is used both when rejoining the colon is unsafe and as a treatment for intestinal obstruction.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: COLECTOMY → COLOSTOMY
Description: A colectomy is the surgical removal of all or part of the colon. A colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening (stoma) from the colon to the outside of the body, allowing stool to be diverted into a collection bag. A colostomy may be performed during a colectomy when it is not safe to reconnect the two healthy ends of the remaining colon. This situation can arise after cancerous tissue is removed, if the remaining sections cannot be safely rejoined. Additionally, a colectomy with colostomy is an option for treating a bowel blockage. Therefore, the combination of these two procedures is used both when rejoining the colon is unsafe and as a treatment for intestinal obstruction.</data>
    </node>
