    <node id="MONITORING">
      <data key="d0">MONITORING</data>
      <data key="d1">MONITORING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">MONITORING refers to the ongoing observation of a patient as part of follow-up care after treatment. It encompasses a follow-up observation strategy designed to watch for cancer recurrence or progression, which may include a watch-and-wait approach or regular testing. This process ensures that any changes in the patient's condition are detected in a timely manner, allowing for appropriate interventions if necessary. Overall, monitoring is a critical component of post-treatment care, focused on surveillance and early detection of any signs of disease returning or advancing.
Relationships:
  - Source: MONITORING
  - Target: FOLLOW-UP TESTING AND SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Monitoring includes follow-up testing and surveillance
  - Source: FOLLOW-UP CARE
  - Target: MONITORING
  - Description: Follow-up care includes monitoring after treatment is over.
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: MONITORING
  - Description: Monitoring is an option if a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery
  - Source: LOBECTOMY
  - Target: MONITORING
  - Description: After lobectomy for less invasive cancer, monitoring is an alternative to additional surgery
  - Source: MONITORING
  - Target: WATCH-AND-WAIT APPROACH
  - Description: Watch-and-wait is a form of monitoring

---</data>
