    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that merges positron emission tomography (PET) with computed tomography (CT). It uses the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), also known as F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose, to detect cancer by showing glucose uptake in cells. Patients must not eat or drink for at least 4 hours before the scan. This imaging test is used to evaluate cancer and can identify malignant involvement in primary sites, lymph nodes, and/or distant sites. It is particularly useful when other imaging results are unclear, and the scan can be performed at the same time as a diagnostic CT. In specific clinical contexts, FDG-PET/CT is used as a follow-up imaging scan from the neck to the midthigh, usually performed 3 to 6 months after treatment for certain cervical cancer stages. Additionally, it may be used when leukemia outside the blood and bone marrow is suspected. The combination of PET and CT provides both metabolic and anatomic information, aiding in the detection and evaluation of cancer.
Relationships:
  - Source: PET/CT SCAN
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT is a specific type of PET/CT scan. A PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) into a single imaging procedure. FDG-PET/CT specifically uses a radiotracer called fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), which is a glucose analog. This radiotracer is injected into the body, and the PET/CT scanner detects its distribution to evaluate metabolic activity, often used in oncology, cardiology, and neurology. Thus, FDG-PET/CT is defined as a PET/CT scan that employs fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) as its radiotracer. In essence, FDG-PET/CT is a PET/CT scan using FDG, making it a specialized application of the broader PET/CT imaging technique.
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT is a type of imaging used for follow-up of cervical cancer, scanning from the neck to the midthigh.
  - Source: CT SCAN
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: CT SCAN is a diagnostic imaging technique that provides detailed cross-sectional images of the body. FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that integrates a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with CT imaging in a single procedure. Specifically, FDG-PET/CT leverages the strengths of both techniques: PET provides functional information about metabolic activity, while CT provides anatomical detail. This combined approach can be performed at the same time as a CT used for diagnosis, meaning the CT component serves a dual role—it contributes to the fused PET/CT images and also supplies diagnostic-quality CT data. There is no contradiction between these descriptions; rather, they highlight that FDG-PET/CT is a hybrid imaging system that allows simultaneous acquisition of functional and structural information, and that the CT portion can be used for diagnostic evaluation in the same session.
  - Source: PET SCAN
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT is a specialized imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with computed tomography (CT). As a type of PET/CT scan, it inherently includes a PET scan component, which is integrated with CT to provide both functional and anatomical information. The technique utilizes a radiotracer—specifically fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)—to detect glucose uptake, which is particularly useful for identifying cancer cells due to their heightened metabolic activity. In summary, FDG-PET/CT is a PET scan combined with CT that leverages FDG to visualize glucose metabolism, making it a valuable tool in oncology and other medical applications.
  - Source: CT SCAN
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT SCAN
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT combines a PET scan with a CT scan.
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT is an imaging test that belongs to the broader category of imaging tests. Specifically, it is a type of PET/CT imaging test, and it is described at the end of the text.
  - Source: RADIOLOGIST
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: The radiologist interprets FDG-PET/CT scans.
  - Source: FDG-PET/CT
  - Target: BONE SCAN
  - Description: If FDG-PET/CT clearly shows cancer in the bone, a bone scan may not be needed.
  - Source: FDG-PET/CT
  - Target: CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT combines positron emission tomography with computed tomography (CT) imaging.
  - Source: CT
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT can be done at the same time as a CT used for diagnosis.
  - Source: FDG-PET/CT
  - Target: SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT and sodium fluoride PET/CT are two imaging techniques used to evaluate cancer, particularly in bone. When FDG-PET/CT clearly demonstrates the presence of cancer in the bone, a subsequent sodium fluoride PET/CT scan may not be required. In other words, if the FDG-PET/CT already provides unambiguous evidence of bone involvement, the additional information from a sodium fluoride PET/CT is considered unnecessary. This guidance applies to both modalities, emphasizing that a clear positive result on FDG-PET/CT can eliminate the need for further sodium fluoride PET/CT imaging in the same clinical context.
  - Source: CT (COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY) SCAN
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT includes CT imaging as part of the PET/CT procedure.
  - Source: TREATMENT PLANNING
  - Target: FDG-PET/CT
  - Description: FDG-PET/CT is an as-needed test in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
