    <node id="PET">
      <data key="d0">PET</data>
      <data key="d1">PET</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">PET (Positron Emission Tomography) is an imaging test used in the initial evaluation of cervical cancer. It may be performed as part of the initial imaging workup for individuals diagnosed with cervical cancer. The primary purpose of PET in this setting is to determine the extent of the disease before treatment begins, helping to establish how far the cancer has spread. By providing this information, PET assists clinicians in staging cervical cancer and planning appropriate treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: PET
  - Description: PET (Positron Emission Tomography) is an initial imaging modality. Specifically, PET is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging. As part of the IMAGING field, PET serves as a front-line diagnostic tool in the evaluation of cervical cancer, helping to determine the extent of disease at the time of staging.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: PET
Description: PET (Positron Emission Tomography) is an imaging test used in the initial evaluation of cervical cancer. It may be performed as part of the initial imaging workup for individuals diagnosed with cervical cancer. The primary purpose of PET in this setting is to determine the extent of the disease before treatment begins, helping to establish how far the cancer has spread. By providing this information, PET assists clinicians in staging cervical cancer and planning appropriate treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: PET
  - Description: PET (Positron Emission Tomography) is an initial imaging modality. Specifically, PET is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging. As part of the IMAGING field, PET serves as a front-line diagnostic tool in the evaluation of cervical cancer, helping to determine the extent of disease at the time of staging.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT SCAN">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d1">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">PET/CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with a computed tomography (CT) scan into a single examination. This combined imaging technique integrates PET and CT to provide both metabolic information and anatomical detail in one scan, producing more comprehensive and detailed cross-sectional images than either method could achieve alone.

The PET portion of the scan detects metabolic activity, showing how tissues and organs are functioning at a cellular level, while the CT component provides precise anatomical structure and localization. By fusing these two types of data, the PET/CT scan allows physicians to pinpoint areas of abnormal metabolic activity within their exact anatomical context. This makes it particularly valuable for oncology, where it is used to detect, characterize, and monitor various cancers. For example, a combined PET and CT scan can create detailed images to find cancer that has spread outside the bladder, and it can also identify cancer in the prostate as well as its spread to a pelvic lymph node. In this way, the procedure helps in staging cancers and guiding treatment decisions by revealing both the location and the metabolic activity of malignant lesions. The result is a more detailed and accurate image than a PET scan or CT scan performed separately, making PET/CT a powerful tool in modern medical diagnostics.
Relationships:
  - Source: CT SCAN
  - Target: PET/CT SCAN
  - Description: A CT scan is an imaging modality that provides detailed anatomical information. A PET/CT scan is a hybrid imaging procedure that combines CT imaging with positron emission tomography (PET) scanning. The PET/CT scan includes a CT component, which is used to provide anatomical localization and attenuation correction for the functional data obtained from the PET portion. In essence, a PET/CT scan combines a PET scan with a CT scan into a single examination, allowing for both metabolic activity and structural detail to be assessed together.
  - Source: PET/CT SCAN
  - Target: PET SCAN
  - Description: A PET/CT scan combines PET scanning with CT imaging in a single procedure.
  - Source: PET SCAN
  - Target: PET/CT SCAN
  - Description: A PET/CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that combines a PET scan with CT imaging. The term "PET/CT scan" refers to the integration of positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) into a single examination, allowing for both functional and anatomical information to be captured in one session. This combined approach is also described as a PET scan combined with a CT scan. The PET/CT scan is therefore a fusion of PET and CT technologies, providing a comprehensive diagnostic tool. The entity "PET SCAN" is a component of the "PET/CT SCAN," which specifically refers to the combined imaging modality.
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
  - Target: PET/CT SCAN
  - Description: PET/CT is a combined imaging method that falls under imaging tests.
  - Source: RADIOLOGIST
  - Target: PET/CT SCAN
  - Description: The radiologist interprets combined PET/CT imaging studies.
  - Source: CT
  - Target: PET/CT SCAN
  - Description: PET/CT scan is a PET scan combined with CT.
  - Source: PET/CT SCAN
  - Target: SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT
  - Description: Sodium fluoride PET/CT is a form of PET/CT scan.
  - Source: CT (COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY) SCAN
  - Target: PET/CT SCAN
  - Description: A PET/CT scan combines PET with a CT scan.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
