    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: PET/CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: PET/CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: PET/CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">FDG-PET/CT is a specific type of PET/CT scan. A PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) into a single imaging procedure. FDG-PET/CT specifically uses a radiotracer called fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), which is a glucose analog. This radiotracer is injected into the body, and the PET/CT scanner detects its distribution to evaluate metabolic activity, often used in oncology, cardiology, and neurology. Thus, FDG-PET/CT is defined as a PET/CT scan that employs fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) as its radiotracer. In essence, FDG-PET/CT is a PET/CT scan using FDG, making it a specialized application of the broader PET/CT imaging technique.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: Relationship: PET/CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT
