    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: FDG-PET/CT → SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: FDG-PET/CT → SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: FDG-PET/CT → SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">FDG-PET/CT and sodium fluoride PET/CT are two imaging techniques used to evaluate cancer, particularly in bone. When FDG-PET/CT clearly demonstrates the presence of cancer in the bone, a subsequent sodium fluoride PET/CT scan may not be required. In other words, if the FDG-PET/CT already provides unambiguous evidence of bone involvement, the additional information from a sodium fluoride PET/CT is considered unnecessary. This guidance applies to both modalities, emphasizing that a clear positive result on FDG-PET/CT can eliminate the need for further sodium fluoride PET/CT imaging in the same clinical context.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: Relationship: FDG-PET/CT → SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT
Description: FDG-PET/CT and sodium fluoride PET/CT are two imaging techniques used to evaluate cancer, particularly in bone. When FDG-PET/CT clearly demonstrates the presence of cancer in the bone, a subsequent sodium fluoride PET/CT scan may not be required. In other words, if the FDG-PET/CT already provides unambiguous evidence of bone involvement, the additional information from a sodium fluoride PET/CT is considered unnecessary. This guidance applies to both modalities, emphasizing that a clear positive result on FDG-PET/CT can eliminate the need for further sodium fluoride PET/CT imaging in the same clinical context.</data>
    </node>
