    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: PET SCAN → PET/CT SCAN">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: PET SCAN → PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: PET SCAN → PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A PET/CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that combines a PET scan with CT imaging. The term "PET/CT scan" refers to the integration of positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) into a single examination, allowing for both functional and anatomical information to be captured in one session. This combined approach is also described as a PET scan combined with a CT scan. The PET/CT scan is therefore a fusion of PET and CT technologies, providing a comprehensive diagnostic tool. The entity "PET SCAN" is a component of the "PET/CT SCAN," which specifically refers to the combined imaging modality.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: Relationship: PET SCAN → PET/CT SCAN
Description: A PET/CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that combines a PET scan with CT imaging. The term "PET/CT scan" refers to the integration of positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) into a single examination, allowing for both functional and anatomical information to be captured in one session. This combined approach is also described as a PET scan combined with a CT scan. The PET/CT scan is therefore a fusion of PET and CT technologies, providing a comprehensive diagnostic tool. The entity "PET SCAN" is a component of the "PET/CT SCAN," which specifically refers to the combined imaging modality.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">CT SCAN is a diagnostic imaging technique that provides detailed cross-sectional images of the body. FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that integrates a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with CT imaging in a single procedure. Specifically, FDG-PET/CT leverages the strengths of both techniques: PET provides functional information about metabolic activity, while CT provides anatomical detail. This combined approach can be performed at the same time as a CT used for diagnosis, meaning the CT component serves a dual role—it contributes to the fused PET/CT images and also supplies diagnostic-quality CT data. There is no contradiction between these descriptions; rather, they highlight that FDG-PET/CT is a hybrid imaging system that allows simultaneous acquisition of functional and structural information, and that the CT portion can be used for diagnostic evaluation in the same session.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: Relationship: CT SCAN → FDG-PET/CT
Description: CT SCAN is a diagnostic imaging technique that provides detailed cross-sectional images of the body. FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that integrates a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with CT imaging in a single procedure. Specifically, FDG-PET/CT leverages the strengths of both techniques: PET provides functional information about metabolic activity, while CT provides anatomical detail. This combined approach can be performed at the same time as a CT used for diagnosis, meaning the CT component serves a dual role—it contributes to the fused PET/CT images and also supplies diagnostic-quality CT data. There is no contradiction between these descriptions; rather, they highlight that FDG-PET/CT is a hybrid imaging system that allows simultaneous acquisition of functional and structural information, and that the CT portion can be used for diagnostic evaluation in the same session.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: CT SCAN → PET/CT SCAN">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: CT SCAN → PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: CT SCAN → PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A CT scan is an imaging modality that provides detailed anatomical information. A PET/CT scan is a hybrid imaging procedure that combines CT imaging with positron emission tomography (PET) scanning. The PET/CT scan includes a CT component, which is used to provide anatomical localization and attenuation correction for the functional data obtained from the PET portion. In essence, a PET/CT scan combines a PET scan with a CT scan into a single examination, allowing for both metabolic activity and structural detail to be assessed together.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: Relationship: CT SCAN → PET/CT SCAN
Description: A CT scan is an imaging modality that provides detailed anatomical information. A PET/CT scan is a hybrid imaging procedure that combines CT imaging with positron emission tomography (PET) scanning. The PET/CT scan includes a CT component, which is used to provide anatomical localization and attenuation correction for the functional data obtained from the PET portion. In essence, a PET/CT scan combines a PET scan with a CT scan into a single examination, allowing for both metabolic activity and structural detail to be assessed together.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: PET SCAN → FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: PET SCAN → FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: PET SCAN → FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">FDG-PET/CT is a specialized imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with computed tomography (CT). As a type of PET/CT scan, it inherently includes a PET scan component, which is integrated with CT to provide both functional and anatomical information. The technique utilizes a radiotracer—specifically fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)—to detect glucose uptake, which is particularly useful for identifying cancer cells due to their heightened metabolic activity. In summary, FDG-PET/CT is a PET scan combined with CT that leverages FDG to visualize glucose metabolism, making it a valuable tool in oncology and other medical applications.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: Relationship: PET SCAN → FDG-PET/CT
Description: FDG-PET/CT is a specialized imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with computed tomography (CT). As a type of PET/CT scan, it inherently includes a PET scan component, which is integrated with CT to provide both functional and anatomical information. The technique utilizes a radiotracer—specifically fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)—to detect glucose uptake, which is particularly useful for identifying cancer cells due to their heightened metabolic activity. In summary, FDG-PET/CT is a PET scan combined with CT that leverages FDG to visualize glucose metabolism, making it a valuable tool in oncology and other medical applications.</data>
    </node>
