    <node id="TURBT">
      <data key="d0">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d1">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">TURBT, or transurethral resection of bladder tumor, is a surgical procedure used to diagnose and treat bladder cancer. During the procedure, a surgeon examines the bladder wall through the urethra and removes visible tumors. TURBT also provides a tissue sample of the bladder wall for diagnostic purposes, helping confirm the presence and type of cancer.

As a treatment event, TURBT is a key component of trimodal therapy, where it is used to remove all visible bladder cancer. After the initial TURBT procedure, follow-up treatments are typically scheduled to destroy any remaining cancer cells. These follow-up approaches may include intravesical therapy—delivering medication directly into the bladder—or chemotherapy, both of which are discussed in relation to TURBT as part of a comprehensive bladder cancer management plan.
Relationships:
  - Source: TURBT
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is scheduled after TURBT and at intervals after the procedure.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: TURBT
  - Description: Intravesical therapy and TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor) are two treatments commonly used together for bladder cancer. TURBT is a surgical procedure that removes visible tumors from the bladder lining. Intravesical therapy involves delivering anticancer drugs directly into the bladder. It is often performed in combination with TURBT, and its primary purpose is to destroy cancer cells that TURBT left behind, helping to reduce the risk of recurrence and eliminate residual disease. Together, these treatments provide a combined approach: TURBT removes the main tumor mass, while intravesical therapy targets remaining microscopic cancer cells.
  - Source: TURBT
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical therapy is given after TURBT to destroy any remaining cancer cells and prevent new cancer cell formation.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG
  - Target: TURBT
  - Description: Intravesical BCG is usually started 3 to 6 weeks after TURBT and may be continued as maintenance after TURBT.</data>
