    <node id="HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) is a medical treatment that refers to the use of hormones to supplement or replace the body’s natural hormone production. In the context of menopause, HRT was formerly the name for menopausal hormone therapy and is used to lessen the side effects of radiation-induced menopause. This treatment may also be suggested for symptoms of surgical menopause—menopause that occurs after the surgical removal of the ovaries. In both of these gynecological contexts, the therapy is commonly abbreviated as HRT.

In a distinct but related application, hormone replacement therapy also refers to the administration of levothyroxine after thyroid surgery. This treatment is used to keep thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels low or normal, helping to prevent recurrence of thyroid disease and maintain metabolic balance. After a total thyroidectomy, lifelong levothyroxine therapy is required to replace the thyroid hormone that the body can no longer produce.

Thus, hormone replacement therapy encompasses both menopausal hormone therapy (historically called HRT) and thyroid hormone replacement, with the abbreviation HRT used across these different medical situations.
Relationships:
  - Source: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY
  - Target: BLOOD TESTS
  - Description: Blood tests measure TSH, which guides hormone replacement therapy.
  - Source: TOTAL THYROIDECTOMY
  - Target: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY
  - Description: Lifelong thyroid hormone replacement therapy is needed after total thyroidectomy.
  - Source: LOBECTOMY
  - Target: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY
  - Description: Levothyroxine may be given after lobectomy to keep TSH low or normal, though it is not always needed.
  - Source: PREMATURE MENOPAUSE
  - Target: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY
  - Description: Hormone replacement therapy may be suggested to manage symptoms of premature/surgical menopause.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
