    <edge source="CANCER" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cervical cancer is a specific type of cancer that originates in the cervix, which is the lower part of the uterus connecting to the vagina. As a distinct form of the broader disease category known as cancer, cervical cancer is characterized by the uncontrolled growth and division of abnormal cells in the cervical tissue. Being classified as a type of cancer means that it shares the fundamental features of cancer, such as the potential to invade nearby tissues and spread to other parts of the body if not detected and treated early. Cervical cancer is most commonly caused by persistent infection with high-risk strains of the human papillomavirus (HPV), though this detail is not explicitly mentioned in the provided descriptions, it is a well-known aspect of the condition. The descriptions emphasize that cervical cancer is a specific cancer type, occurring in the cervix, and is not a general term but a precise diagnosis within oncology. Thus, cervical cancer falls under the overarching classification of cancer, but it is distinguished by its anatomical site of origin and its unique biological behavior, risk factors, and prevention strategies. In summary, cervical cancer is a type of cancer that arises in the cervix, making it one of the many site-specific cancers within the broader category of malignant diseases. This concise definition captures the essential relationship between the general disease entity, cancer, and this particular manifestation, cervical cancer. The information from all descriptions is consistent and non-contradictory, reinforcing that cervical cancer is both a type of cancer and specifically localized to the cervix. Therefore, the comprehensive description of cervical cancer is that it is a malignant neoplasm of the cervix, classified under the umbrella term cancer, with all the implications that such a classification carries for diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
