    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team is central to the entire cancer treatment process, particularly for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Their responsibilities begin with planning the next steps of care, which are determined based on imaging and test results. Using these findings, the care team identifies the appropriate course of action and outlines the treatment pathway.

The care team provides treatment options and comprehensive care to the patient. Treatment planning is a collaborative process: the patient and care team together choose a treatment plan that aligns with the patients needs and preferences. Once a plan is agreed upon, the care team coordinates care throughout the entire treatment journey, ensuring that all aspects of the plan are implemented smoothly.

In addition to planning, the care team actively prescribes and monitors treatments. They ensure that each treatment is properly initiated, and they list the start date of each treatment. This monitoring is essential for tracking the patients response and adjusting the plan as needed. Ultimately, the care team provides the planned cancer treatment itself, delivering the therapies that have been selected for the patient. Through this combination of planning, prescribing, monitoring, coordinating, and providing care, the care team manages the full continuum of AML treatment, supporting the patient from initial decision-making through ongoing therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is a form of medical treatment used primarily in oncology. As a cancer treatment, chemotherapy involves the use of cytotoxic drugs to destroy or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It is applicable across multiple cancer types and is often integrated into broader therapeutic protocols. Specifically, chemotherapy is a recognized type of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it serves as the standard-of-care approach for managing this blood cancer. In addition to its role in AML, chemotherapy is also a component of cervical cancer treatment, frequently administered as part of chemoradiationa combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy that enhances the effectiveness of treatment. As such, chemotherapy occupies a central position within the overall category of cancer treatment, functioning both as a standalone modality and as an adjunct to other therapies. The entity TREATMENT encompasses a wide range of medical interventions, and chemotherapy is one of its key subtypes, distinguished by its mechanism of action and its established role in hematologic and solid tumor malignancies. Overall, chemotherapy exemplifies a targeted and evidence-based treatment option, with particular significance in AML, where it is the established standard of care, and in cervical cancer, where it is integrated into combined-modality regimens.</data>
      <data key="d12">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Radiation therapy is a type of treatment that falls under the broader category of medical treatments. Specifically, it is a type of cancer treatment, used to address malignant diseases. Additionally, radiation therapy is a type of treatment for cervical cancer, indicating its application in managing that particular form of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
