    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A biopsy is a medical procedure in which a sample of tissue is taken from the body for examination, and it is required to diagnose ovarian cancer. This procedure is performed not only to confirm the presence of ovarian cancer but also to determine its specific type and grade, which are essential for guiding treatment decisions. Thus, the biopsy serves as the definitive diagnostic tool for ovarian cancer, providing critical information about the disease's characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A biopsy is a medical procedure used to confirm a diagnosis of prostate cancer. It is the definitive method for diagnosing the disease, as it allows healthcare providers to obtain tissue samples from the prostate gland for pathological examination. In the context of prostate cancer, a biopsy serves to verify the presence of cancerous cells, thereby confirming or ruling out the condition. Both descriptions agree that the primary role of a biopsy is diagnostic confirmation. Therefore, when prostate cancer is suspectedbased on elevated prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels, abnormal digital rectal exam findings, or imaging resultsa biopsy is the essential next step to establish a definitive diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy results are used to determine cancer stage treatment plan</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Diagnostic testing may lead to a biopsy if cancer is suspected</data>
      <data key="d12">TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="PSA TEST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A PSA test measures the level of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, and an unusually high PSA level may serve as a warning sign of possible prostate cancer. When such an elevated result is detected, it can indicate that further evaluation is needed, often leading to a biopsy to confirm or rule out a diagnosis. In this context, the PSA test acts as an initial screening tool, while a biopsy provides definitive tissue analysis when the PSA level suggests a potential problem.</data>
      <data key="d12">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="OPEN BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">OPEN BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Open biopsy is a type of biopsy, which is a medical procedure used to obtain tissue samples for diagnosis. Specifically, open biopsy is recognized as one of the standard types of biopsy for gliomas, a form of brain tumor. Thus, within the broader category of biopsy, open biopsy holds particular importance in the diagnostic evaluation of gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d12">OPEN BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
