    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING TESTS" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">IMAGING TESTS encompass a range of diagnostic modalities, and ULTRASOUND is one such imaging test. In the context of prostate cancer, imaging methods include ultrasound as a diagnostic tool. Additionally, ultrasound is often the first imaging test used for ovarian cancer. Thus, ultrasound serves as a versatile imaging technique within the broader category of imaging tests, playing a key role in the evaluation of both prostate and ovarian cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING TESTS" target="PET SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d8">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to locate cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">People with certain inherited genetic mutations are at greater risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="EVERYONE WITH A PROSTATE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">EVERYONE WITH A PROSTATE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Everyone with a prostate is at risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">EVERYONE WITH A PROSTATE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Prostate cancer is a disease that originates in the prostate gland. It can be categorized into different stages based on how far it has progressed. Early-stage prostate cancer is one such category or stage of prostate cancer. This stage is defined by the fact that the cancer has not spread beyond the prostate gland. In other words, early-stage prostate cancer remains localized within the prostate, with no evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues or distant metastasis. As a subset of prostate cancer, early-stage prostate cancer represents a less advanced form of the disease, often associated with a better prognosis and more treatment options than later stages. Thus, while prostate cancer encompasses all stages of the disease, early-stage prostate cancer specifically refers to the confined, localized phase.</data>
      <data key="d12">EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Prostate cancer can advance by metastasis, a process defined as the spread of cancer cells outside the prostate to other organs and tissues. This form of progression is characteristic of advanced-stage prostate cancer. Metastasis occurs when cancer cells travel through the bloodstream or the lymphatic system, enabling the cancer to disseminate beyond the prostate to distant sites in the body. In this way, prostate cancer metastasis involves the movement of malignant cells from the prostate via circulatory or lymphatic routes, ultimately establishing secondary growths in other organs and tissues.</data>
      <data key="d12">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy samples are examined to determine whether prostate cancer is present.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="WHITE MALES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">WHITE MALES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">White males are the comparison group for prostate cancer risk; Black males have higher rates of diagnosis and death than white males.</data>
      <data key="d12">WHITE MALES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scans are diagnostic imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d12">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team may use CT when MRI is unavailable or not recommended.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scan is an imaging test used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d12">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="MRI SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scans and MRI scans are both medical imaging tests used to visualize internal structures of the body, but they differ in several key aspects. MRI scans take longer to perform than CT scans, making the overall duration of an MRI exam generally more time-consuming. Additionally, the types of contrast material used for CT and MRI are different; CT scans typically use iodine-based contrast agents, whereas MRI scans commonly use gadolinium-based contrast agents. These differences in contrast materials reflect the distinct imaging technologies and the specific diagnostic information each scan provides.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="UROGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">UROGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A urogram can be performed using CT to evaluate the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d12">UROGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="FDG PET-CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">FDG PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FDG PET-CT can be done at the same time as a CT scan used for diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d12">CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A CT scan (computed tomography) is a medical imaging technique that can reveal suspicious areas within the body. When such an area is identified, a biopsy may be needed to sample the tissue for diagnosis. In this context, CT can also be used to guide the biopsy procedure, helping clinicians precisely target the suspicious region. Together, CT scanning and biopsy are complementary: the CT scan locates and characterizes the area of concern, while the biopsy, often guided by CT, obtains a tissue sample for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="LYMPH NODE BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">LYMPH NODE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scan may be used to guide a lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d12">LYMPH NODE BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="FDG-PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT SCAN is a diagnostic imaging technique that provides detailed cross-sectional images of the body. FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that integrates a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with CT imaging in a single procedure. Specifically, FDG-PET/CT leverages the strengths of both techniques: PET provides functional information about metabolic activity, while CT provides anatomical detail. This combined approach can be performed at the same time as a CT used for diagnosis, meaning the CT component serves a dual roleit contributes to the fused PET/CT images and also supplies diagnostic-quality CT data. There is no contradiction between these descriptions; rather, they highlight that FDG-PET/CT is a hybrid imaging system that allows simultaneous acquisition of functional and structural information, and that the CT portion can be used for diagnostic evaluation in the same session.</data>
      <data key="d12">FDG-PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="FDG-PET/CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">FDG-PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FDG-PET/CT combines a PET scan with a CT scan</data>
      <data key="d12">FDG-PET/CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="PET/CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A CT scan is an imaging modality that provides detailed anatomical information. A PET/CT scan is a hybrid imaging procedure that combines CT imaging with positron emission tomography (PET) scanning. The PET/CT scan includes a CT component, which is used to provide anatomical localization and attenuation correction for the functional data obtained from the PET portion. In essence, a PET/CT scan combines a PET scan with a CT scan into a single examination, allowing for both metabolic activity and structural detail to be assessed together.</data>
      <data key="d12">PET/CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
