    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="SURGERY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Radiation therapy and surgery are two distinct treatment modalities that are often used in combination for cancer care. Surgery is used to physically remove tumors, while radiation therapy uses high-energy radiation to destroy cancer cells. In many cases, treatment plans integrate both approaches: after surgery, radiation therapy is commonly administered to decrease the chance of the tumor returning and to kill any remaining cancer cells that were not removed during the operation. Additionally, radiation therapy serves as an alternative treatment option for patients who cannot undergo surgery or who choose not to have it.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
