    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging describes whether cancer has metastasized to distant organs such as the lungs or liver.</data>
      <data key="d12">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="TNM SCORES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">TNM SCORES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging uses TNM scores for tumor, node, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d12">TNM SCORES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL STAGING" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical staging is a process used to determine the extent of cancer within the body, and it relies on a combination of diagnostic information. Biopsy results play a central role in clinical staging, providing essential pathological confirmation of the disease. However, biopsy findings alone are not sufficient; clinical staging also incorporates data from a physical exam and other tests, including imaging studies. In practice, clinical staging is based on biopsy results in addition to a thorough physical examination and imaging tests, ensuring that the stage reflects both the microscopic characteristics of the tissue and the macroscopic evidence of disease spread. This comprehensive approach helps clinicians accurately assess the prognosis and plan appropriate treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL STAGING" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical staging is a process used to determine the extent and spread of a disease, typically cancer, before treatment begins. It relies on imaging tests, which are essential tools for assessing the stage of the condition. Imaging testssuch as X-rays, CT scans, MRIs, PET scans, and ultrasoundare performed prior to treatment to provide detailed information about the location, size, and involvement of affected tissues or organs. These tests form the foundation of clinical staging, as they allow healthcare providers to evaluate the disease without invasive procedures. In summary, clinical staging is based on imaging tests, and such imaging tests are specifically used in clinical staging before treatment to guide diagnosis, prognosis, and therapeutic planning. The relationship between clinical staging and imaging tests is integral: imaging tests supply the visual and functional data that clinicians use to classify the stage of the disease, which in turn informs treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL STAGING" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical staging is based on a physical exam</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL STAGING" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical staging is the pre-treatment portion of cancer staging. Cancer staging is often performed as clinical staging before treatment begins.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL STAGING" target="PATHOLOGIC STAGING" id="related_to">
