    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), also referred to simply as IMAGING in certain contexts, is a type of imaging that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed pictures of the body's internal structures. As an imaging test, MRI provides highly detailed images without the use of ionizing radiation, making it a valuable diagnostic tool.</data>
      <data key="d12">MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging is used after cervical cancer treatment to detect recurrence or spread.</data>
      <data key="d12">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">IMAGING and BIOPSY are complementary procedures in the management of glioma and other cancers, though the order in which they are used depends on the clinical context. In cases where a biopsy has already confirmed disease progression, IMAGING is the next step to determine whether the cancer is locally progressive or has become metastatic. This helps guide further treatment by clarifying the extent and spread of the disease. Conversely, when initial IMAGING findings suggest the presence of a glioma, a BIOPSY is then performed as the next step to confirm the diagnosis. In this scenario, imaging serves as the screening or detection tool that prompts tissue sampling for pathological confirmation. Thus, the relationship between IMAGING and BIOPSY is bidirectional: imaging can lead to biopsy when a suspicious lesion is found, and biopsy can lead to imaging when progression is confirmed, each serving distinct roles in diagnosis and disease monitoring. Together, they form an integrated diagnostic and surveillance pathway for brain tumors and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="FOLLOW-UP IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">FOLLOW-UP IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Follow-up imaging is imaging performed after treatment when recurrence or spread is suspected</data>
      <data key="d12">FOLLOW-UP IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging is an initial testing method to determine the extent of cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="FDG-PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FDG-PET/CT is a type of imaging used for follow-up of cervical cancer, scanning from the neck to the midthigh.</data>
      <data key="d12">FDG-PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">IMAGING encompasses various techniques used to visualize internal structures and detect disease. Among these, PET/CT is a more advanced imaging test that may be needed to detect cancer spread farther than lymph nodes. PET/CT is an imaging test that combines positron emission tomography (PET) with computed tomography (CT), allowing for both functional and anatomical assessment in a single procedure.</data>
      <data key="d12">PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PET" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PET</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET (Positron Emission Tomography) is an initial imaging modality. Specifically, PET is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging. As part of the IMAGING field, PET serves as a front-line diagnostic tool in the evaluation of cervical cancer, helping to determine the extent of disease at the time of staging.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PSA TEST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A PSA test is often paired with imaging to get a more complete picture of prostate health</data>
      <data key="d12">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Transvaginal ultrasound is an initial imaging modality within the broader field of imaging. Specifically, it is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging, serving as a key diagnostic tool for evaluating the extent of disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ultrasound is a type of imaging modality used to visualize internal structures of the body. As an imaging technique, it falls under the broader category of diagnostic imaging, which encompasses various methods for producing images of the human body for clinical analysis and medical intervention. Specifically, ultrasound is classified as a type of imaging test, meaning it is a diagnostic procedure that utilizes high-frequency sound waves to generate real-time images of organs, tissues, and blood flow, without the use of ionizing radiation. In the context of the relationships between these entities, IMAGING serves as the overarching category, while ULTRASOUND is a specific subset or application within that category. Thus, ultrasound is both a form of imaging and a distinct imaging test used in medical practice.</data>
      <data key="d12">ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging, specifically computed tomography (CT), serves as an initial imaging modality and is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging. Additionally, CT with contrast is an imaging option for follow-up of cervical cancer stages 2, 3, and 4A.</data>
      <data key="d12">CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGICAL STAGING" target="HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO" id="related_to">
