    <edge source="PSA DENSITY" target="MRI SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PSA DENSITY</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PSA density is a clinical metric calculated using prostate size, which is measured via an MRI scan. The MRI scan provides an accurate measurement of prostate volume, and this measurement is then used in conjunction with the serum PSA (prostate-specific antigen) level to derive the PSA density. Thus, the MRI scan plays a key role in determining PSA density, which helps in assessing prostate-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA DENSITY" target="TRANSECTAL ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PSA DENSITY</data>
      <data key="d8">TRANSECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PSA density is a clinical measurement that is calculated using prostate size, which is determined via transrectal ultrasound. Transrectal ultrasound provides an accurate assessment of prostate volume, and this measurement is then used to compute PSA density, helping to contextualize PSA levels in relation to gland size.</data>
      <data key="d12">TRANSECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
