    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="LIVER CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Primary liver cancer is cancer that starts in the liver, meaning it is a specific category of liver cancer. It is distinct from cancers that spread to the liver from other parts of the body. As a type of liver cancer, primary liver cancer originates within the liver itself, and it falls under the broader classification of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA (HCC)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA (HCC)</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">HCC is the most common type of primary liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA (HCC)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common type of primary liver cancer in adults.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="INTRAHEPATIC CHOLANGIOCARCINOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAHEPATIC CHOLANGIOCARCINOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common type of primary liver cancer in adults.</data>
      <data key="d12">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="SARCOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">SARCOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Rare sarcomas that start in blood vessel cells are a type of primary liver cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">SARCOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" target="MIXED-TYPE TUMOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">MIXED-TYPE TUMOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A mixed-type tumor is a rare type of primary liver cancer containing both hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d12">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SECONDARY LIVER CANCER" target="LIVER CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SECONDARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Secondary liver cancer is a form of liver cancer that occurs when cancer cells from another part of the body spread, or metastasize, to the liver. It is not a cancer that originates in the liver itself, but rather a secondary site of a primary cancer located elsewhere. As such, secondary liver cancer is categorized under the broader term "liver cancer," which encompasses both primary liver cancer (starting in the liver) and secondary liver cancer (spreading to the liver from other organs). The development of secondary liver cancer indicates that the original cancer has advanced and disseminated through the bloodstream or lymphatic system. Treatment and prognosis depend on the source of the primary cancer, the extent of liver involvement, and overall patient health.</data>
      <data key="d12">LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHRONIC HEPATITIS B" target="LIVER CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHRONIC HEPATITIS B</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chronic hepatitis B is a risk factor for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHRONIC HEPATITIS B</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHRONIC HEPATITIS B" target="LIVER CANCER SCREENING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHRONIC HEPATITIS B</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER SCREENING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">People with chronic hepatitis B should be enrolled in a liver cancer screening program.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHRONIC HEPATITIS B</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTATIC LIVER CANCER" target="LIVER CANCER" id="related_to">
