    <edge source="LARYNGOSCOPY" target="DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d8">DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Laryngoscopy is a medical procedure used to visualize the inside of the larynx (voice box) and nearby structures of the throat. Direct laryngoscopy is a specific type of laryngoscopy. In direct laryngoscopy, the practitioner inserts a rigid or flexible scope directly into the airway to obtain a clear view of the larynx, often for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes. Both descriptions confirm that direct laryngoscopy falls under the broader category of laryngoscopy, making it one of the methods within this diagnostic and interventional technique.</data>
      <data key="d12">LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LARYNGOSCOPY" target="FIBEROPTIC LARYNGOSCOPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d8">FIBEROPTIC LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Laryngoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the larynx (voice box) and surrounding structures. Fiberoptic laryngoscopy is a type of laryngoscopy that utilizes a flexible fiberoptic scope to visualize the larynx. In other words, fiberoptic laryngoscopy falls under the broader category of laryngoscopy, sharing the same diagnostic and interventional purpose but distinguished by its use of fiberoptic technology. Together, these terms describe a continuum of techniques for viewing the throat and airway, with fiberoptic laryngoscopy representing a specific, commonly used variation of the standard laryngoscopic approach.</data>
      <data key="d12">FIBEROPTIC LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LARYNGOSCOPY" target="NASOLARYNGOSCOPY" id="related_to">
