    <edge source="FDG-PET/CT" target="CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d8">CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FDG-PET/CT combines positron emission tomography with computed tomography (CT) imaging.</data>
      <data key="d12">FDG-PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDG-PET/CT" target="SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d8">SODIUM FLUORIDE PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FDG-PET/CT and sodium fluoride PET/CT are two imaging techniques used to evaluate cancer, particularly in bone. When FDG-PET/CT clearly demonstrates the presence of cancer in the bone, a subsequent sodium fluoride PET/CT scan may not be required. In other words, if the FDG-PET/CT already provides unambiguous evidence of bone involvement, the additional information from a sodium fluoride PET/CT is considered unnecessary. This guidance applies to both modalities, emphasizing that a clear positive result on FDG-PET/CT can eliminate the need for further sodium fluoride PET/CT imaging in the same clinical context.</data>
      <data key="d12">FDG-PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
