    <edge source="PET/CT SCAN" target="FDG-PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FDG-PET/CT is a specific type of PET/CT scan. A PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) into a single imaging procedure. FDG-PET/CT specifically uses a radiotracer called fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), which is a glucose analog. This radiotracer is injected into the body, and the PET/CT scanner detects its distribution to evaluate metabolic activity, often used in oncology, cardiology, and neurology. Thus, FDG-PET/CT is defined as a PET/CT scan that employs fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) as its radiotracer. In essence, FDG-PET/CT is a PET/CT scan using FDG, making it a specialized application of the broader PET/CT imaging technique.</data>
      <data key="d12">FDG-PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
