    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical BCG therapy is a form of intravesical therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL BCG" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical BCG is a type of intravesical therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="TURBT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical therapy and TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor) are two treatments commonly used together for bladder cancer. TURBT is a surgical procedure that removes visible tumors from the bladder lining. Intravesical therapy involves delivering anticancer drugs directly into the bladder. It is often performed in combination with TURBT, and its primary purpose is to destroy cancer cells that TURBT left behind, helping to reduce the risk of recurrence and eliminate residual disease. Together, these treatments provide a combined approach: TURBT removes the main tumor mass, while intravesical therapy targets remaining microscopic cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical chemotherapy is a type of intravesical therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical therapy and chemotherapy are both cancer-fighting treatments, but they differ in how the drugs are administered. Intravesical therapy is a localized treatment used primarily for bladder cancer, in which medication is placed directly into the bladder through a catheter. Chemotherapy, in its common systemic form, involves cancer-fighting drugs given throughout the body by infusion, usually through a vein, so that the drugs travel in the bloodstream to reach cancer cells anywhere in the body.

However, the two are closely related in the context of bladder cancer: chemotherapy is actually one of the two main intravesical therapies. This means that chemotherapy drugs can be administered intravesicallydirectly into the bladderrather than systemically. In that form, it is known as intravesical chemotherapy. The other main intravesical therapy is typically immunotherapy (such as BCG), which is not chemotherapy.

Thus, the apparent contradiction is resolved by distinguishing between two modes of chemotherapy: intravesical chemotherapy, where the drugs are placed directly into the bladder as a type of intravesical therapy, and systemic chemotherapy, where the drugs are infused throughout the body. Both intravesical therapy and chemotherapy involve cancer-fighting drugs, but intravesical therapy is always local to the bladder, while chemotherapy can be either local (intravesical) or systemic.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical therapy is a local treatment, whereas systemic therapy treats the whole body; the text contrasts these approaches.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG" target="TURBT" id="related_to">
